4800:
Related to: ** συζῶ**,
_imperative_ σύζηθι Philem.p.69 Demiańczuk:— **live with**, σ. τινί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 580, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.69; μετ᾽ ἀλλήλων 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1156a27:}} with _dative_ _of things_, σ. φιλοπραγμοσύνῃ **pass one's life in.. **, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.14}}; βίῳ αὐχμηρῷ σ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Salt.” 1}}; θηρίον ὕδατι συζῶν a creature **living in** water, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista “Praeparatio Sophistica” p.6 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus}}
**2** _absolutely_, **live together**, [πολιτεία] χαλεπὴ συζῆν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 302b, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1126b11, 1170b11, +others, “Pol.” 1278b21, 1280b38; οἱ συζῶντες [prev. author] “EN” 1157b7.}}

4801:
** συ-ζεύγνῡμι**,
**yoke together, couple**, ἵππους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.189, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.2.26 (_passive_)}}; especially in marriage, τῷ μὲν φίλην σύζευξον ἄλοχον [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 166, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 7.30; σ. νέους καὶ νέας 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1335a16 {συζευκτέον}; τὸν Ἄρη πρὸς τὴν Ἀφροδίτην [prev. work] 1269b28}}]:—_middle_, **yoke for oneself**, ἅρμα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.1.51}}:—_passive_, **to be yoked with, coupled with, paired**, μετ᾽ ἀλλήλων {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 585b9; πρὸς ἀλλήλας 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 8.4.2: _metaphorically_, τῷ συνέζευξαι πλάνῳ}}; variant for{προσ-} in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 482; τὸν ἐμὸν δαίμον᾽, ᾧ συνεζύγην [prev. author] “Andr.” 98}}; τίνι πότμῳ συνεζύγη; {{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. author] “Hel.” 255; οἵᾳ ξυμφορᾷ συνεζύγης [prev. author] “Hipp.” 1389 (conversely, συνέζευκται τὸ πάθος τινί 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.57 W.)}}; συζυγέντες ὁμιλοῦσι they live **in close familiarity**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 2.12.}}
**2** less frequently, also in _passive_, of things, **to be closely united**, ἁ ψυχὰ τῷ σώματι συνέζευκται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Philolaus Philosophus 14; πεμπάδι συζυγείς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 546c; συνέζευκται ἡ φρόνησις τῇ τοῦ ἤθους ἀρετῇ 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1178a16, compare 1175a19; διορίζεται τοῖς συνεζευγμένοις συμπτώμασι 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 16.535.}}

4802:
** συζητ-έω**,
**search** or **examine together with**, τινι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 384c, etc.; τινὶ περί τινος [prev. author] “Men.” 90b}}:—_passive_, **to be discussed**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Demetrius Lacon Philosophus “Herc.” 1006 at Title Page}}
**II** σ. τινί or πρός τινα **dispute** with.., {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.Act.6.9, 9.29, compare “POxy.” 1673.20 (2nd c.AD)}}; σ. πρὸς αὑτούς {{NT|NT.Mark.1.27, NT.Luke.22.23.}}

4803:
** συζήτ-ησις**, εως, ἡ,
**joint inquiry, discussion**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Gnomologium Vaticanum - Kleine Schriften” 74, 1st c.BC: Cicero, M. Tullius Orator et Philosophus “Epistulae ad Familiares” 16.21.4, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.44 W.}}
**II** **disputation**, {{NT+1st c.BC+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.191, compare 113 (_plural_), 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2p.240S.(_plural_), NT.Act.15.7 (variant), etc.}}

4805:
** σύζῠγ-ος**, ον,
**yoked together, paired, united**, especially by marriage, σ. ὁμαυλίαι **wedded** union, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 599 (Lyric poetry)}}; of fish, **swimming in pairs**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 610b8.}}
**2** as _substantive_, _feminine_, **wife**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 314, 342}}; _masculine_, **yoke-fellow, comrade**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “IT” 250, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 945, NT.Phil.4.3 (unless _proper name_)}}; **brother**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 1001}}; a gladiator's **adversary**, Herzog **Koische Forschungen**{{Refs|No. 133, “JHS” 34.19 (from Lycia), “epigram Gr.” 318 (from Smyrna)}}; of things, ἀδελφὰ τούτοις καὶ σ. πεποιηκότες {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.10d.}}
**II** **common**, σ. πᾶσι πατρίς {{Refs|“App.Anth.” 2.26.}} _adverb_ -γως **conjointly with**, τινι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 51.9, etc.}}

4807:
** σῡκᾰμιν-ος**, ἡ,
more rarely ὁ,= μορέα, **mulberry-tree**, ὁ σ. συκάμιν᾽, ὁρᾷς, φορεῖ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Amphis Comicus 38, cf. Menestor cited in 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 5.3.4, “Comica Adespota” 1269, etc.; there were two kinds, red (or black) and white, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 6.6.4, “HP” 1.6.1, +others}}
**II** σ. ἡ Αἰγυπτία,= συκόμορος,{{NT+3rd c.BC+|[prev. work] 1.1.7, compare “PCair.Zen.” 83.3 (3rd c.BC), 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 17.2.4, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.34, NT.Luke.17.6.}}
**III** σ. ἀγρία, ={βάτος} (A), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Phanias Historicus 33.}}

4808:
Included with: ** σῡκέα**, ἡ,
only _Doric dialect_ (see. συκία), _Epic dialect_ _nominative_ _plural_ συκέαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.116, _accusative_ συκέας 24.341 (both _disyllable_)}}; _Ionic dialect_ _accusative_ _singular_ συκέην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.193, 4.23, _genitive_ _plural_ συκέων 1.193}}; elsewhere only in _contraction_ or analogical forms from **συκῆ** (_Epic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_ _nominative_ συκῆ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.246, 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 19, _accusative_ συκῆν 6th c.BC: Hipponax Iambographus 34), “IG” 12.313.163, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 708, etc.}}:—**fig-tree, Ficus Carica**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus only in 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey”; σ. γλυκεραί 7.116}}; for various kinds, see {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 1.3.1, 3.9.3, 4.2.3, 4.4.4, 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 3.74cf}}; ἱερὰ σ. a suburb of Eleusis, where Demeter first produced the fig-tree, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“IG” [same place], 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 1.37.2, 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 3.74d, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vitae Sophistarum” 2.20.3.}}
**2** σ. Αἰγυπτία,= κερωνία, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 1.11.2.}}
**3** σ. περὶ τὴν Ἴδην, **Amelanchier, Amelanchias vulgaris**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 3.17.4.}}
**4** ={χαμαισύκη}, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.169.}}
**5** **banyan, Ficus bengalensis**, {{Refs|Thphr “HP” 4.4.4.}}
**II** ={σῦκον} 1, **fig**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 590.}}
**III** a **tar** or **resin** in Aleppo pine, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 3.9.3, 1st c.AD: Plinius Rerum Naturalium Scriptor “Historia Naturalis” 16.44.}}
**IV** a kind of **spurge**,= πέπλιον, Ps.-{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.168, 1st c.AD: Plinius Rerum Naturalium Scriptor “Historia Naturalis” 27.119.}}
**V** ={σῦκον} {{Refs|11}}**excrescence** on the body, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 2.170, 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 4.203, “Hippiatrica” 82.}}
**VI** a **seaweed**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 4.6.2.}}

4810:
** σῦκον**,
_Boeotian dialect_ **τῦκον** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|(5th c.BC: Strattis Comicus 47.5)}}, τό, **fruit of the** συκῆ, **fig**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.121, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.40, etc.}}; βασίλεια σ. were a large kind, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 241}}; to eat figs in the heat of the day was thought to cause fever, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 80, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 463, 5th-6th c.BC: Nicopho Comicus 12}}; ξηρὰ σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 845b: proverbial, ὅσῳ διαφέρει σῦκα καρδάμων}} 'as different as chalk from cheese', {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Heniochus Comicus 4.2}}; σύκῳ.. σ. οὐδὲ ἓν οὕτως ὅμοιον γέγονεν poetical cited in {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|1st c.BC: Cicero, M. Tullius Orator et Philosophus “Epistulae ad Atticum” 4.8b. 2, compare 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 6.60; τὰ σῦκα σῦκα.. ὀνομάζων 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Hist.Conscr.” 41 (compare σκάφη)}}; σῦκα αἰτεῖν, proverbial for τρυφᾶν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 302 (Lyric poetry)}}; σῦκον χειμῶνος ζητεῖν, of a foolish enterprise, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 11.33.}}
**2** σ. Αἰγύπτιον, **fruit of** κερωνία, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 4.2.4, “Od.” 5.}}
**II** from its shape, **a large wart** on the eyelids, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1247, compare 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 3.7}}; of **tumours** in other places, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 4.200, 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus “Synopsis” 7.40.}}
**III** **pudenda muliebria**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1350.}}

4811:
** σῡκοφαντ-έω**,
**to be a** συκοφάντης 1, παππῷος ὁ βίος συκοφαντεῖν ἐστί μοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1452, compare “Ach.” 828, “Ec.” 562, +others, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 22.1, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.23, 21.5, +others, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 53.1, 55.1, +others, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 1, +others; ς κατ᾽ ἀγοράν 4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 32.16}}: with _accusative_ _person_, **prosecute vexatiously, blackmail**, συκοφαντεῖς τοὺς ξένου; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1431, compare “V.” 1096 (Lyric poetry); τοὺς συμμάχους 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.318; ἑτέρους ἔσειε καὶ ἐσυκοφάντει 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.43; σ. τοὺς τὰς οὐσίας ἔχοντας 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1304b22, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 19.9 (_passive_)}}; συκοφαντοῦμαι νῦν ὑπ᾽ αὐτῶν ἀδίκως {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “fragment” 43, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 11.21, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 23.4; ἰδόντες.. σε ὑπὸ Δημέου συκοφαντούμενον “PMich.Zen.” 57.2 (3rd c.BC),compare “PCair.Zen.” 212.4, 628.3 (3rd c.BC), “CPR” 232.3 (2nd-3rd c.AD)}}; frequently of blackmail by officials, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 43.26, 789.21 (2nd c.BC), “UPZ” 112i4, 113.10, 16 (2nd c.BC)}}; συκοφαντῆσαι ἡμᾶς καὶ διασεῖσαι {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“BGU” 1756.11 (1st c.BC)}}; μηδένα διασείσητε μηδὲ συκοφαντήσητε {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.Luke.3.14, compare “CPR” 238.6 (2nd c.AD), “PFlor.” 382.57 (3rd c.AD)}}; τοῦ συκοφαντῆσαι ἡμᾶς to **seek occasion against** us, **oppress** us, {{LXX|LXX.Gen.43.18}}; ὁ συκοφαντῶν πένητα{{LXX|LXX.Pro.14.31}}; **accuse falsely**, ταυτὶ γὰρ συκοφαντεῖσθαι τὸν Ἕκτορα ὑπὸ τοῦ Ὁμήρου that is **a false charge brought against** Hector by Homer, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Heroicus” 12b; κύριε Γάϊε, συκοφαντούμεθα 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.598, compare 1.145, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 38.28, +others: with _accusative_ et _genitive_, τὸν θεὸν ὀλιγωρίας 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Fragmenta” 40}}: with _accusative_ _of things_, **denounce as contraband**, Μεγαρέων τὰ χλανίσκια {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 519}}; **extort by false charges** or **threats**, τριάκοντα μνᾶς {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 26.24; εἴ τινός τι ἐσυκοφάντησα, ἀποδίδωμι τετραπλοῦν NT.Luke.19.8: _absolutely_, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 18.10.}}
**2** **criticize in a pettifogging way**, τοὺς ποιητάς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1456a5, compare 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Thucydide” 52, “Dem.” 34, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 26.1}}; **lay verbal traps for** one, τὸν ῥήτορα βουλόμενος δικαίως ἐξετάζειν καὶ μὴ σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.232; σ. Θρασύμαχον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 341c}}; ὑποσκελίζειν καὶ σ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.138}}: with _accusative_ _of things_, **quibble about**, μὴ τὰ συμβάντα συκοφάντει {{Refs|[prev. work]192}}; σ. τὸ πρᾶγμα {{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. author] 23.61, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Demosthene” 25}}; **carp at, stint**, τὸν ἐπὶ τοῖς καλλίστοις ἔργοις ἔπαινον {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 4.8}}: _absolutely_, **quibble**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 341b, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 139b26, 157a32, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.62.}}
**II** ={κνίζω ἐρωτικῶς}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 255, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 1071.}}

4812:
** σῡλᾰγωγέω**,
(σῦλον) **carry off as booty, lead captive**, τινα {{NT+3rd c.AD+|NT.Col.2.8, 3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 10.35.}}
**II** **rob, despoil**, τὸν οἶκον {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Aristaenetus Rhetor 2.22.}}

4813:
** σῡλ-άω**,
_imperfect_ _contraction_ in _Epic dialect_ ἐσύλα, σύλα, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.28, 4.116}}; _Ionic dialect_ Iterat. σύλασκε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 480}}:—_passive_, _future_ συληθήσομαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 761, συλήσομαι 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 4.7.10}}:—**strip off**, especially **strip off** the arms of a slain enemy, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus (only in 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad”), Lyric poetry, etc. Construction:}}
**1** in full, with _accusative_ _person_ et _of things_, **strip off from** another, **strip** him **of** his arms (compare σκυλεύω), μή μιν Ἀχαιοὶ τεύχεα συλήσωσι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.428, compare 16.500}}; ἔπειτα δὲ καὶ τὰ (i.e. ἔναρα).. νεκροὺς ἂμ πεδίον συλήσετε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|6.71; με κασίγνητον συλᾷς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 157 (_anapaest meter_); τὴν θεὸν τοὺς στεφάνους σεσυλήκασιν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.182}}:— _passive_, with _accusative_ _of things_, **to be stripped, robbed, deprived of** a thing, σκῆπτρα συληθήσεται {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 761}}; ταῦτ᾽ (i.e. τὰ τόξα) ἐσυλήθην ἐγώ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 413; λέκτρα συλᾶσθαι βίᾳ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1275; συληθεὶς τὰς βοῦς 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 6.19; σεσυλήμεθα τὰ ἡμέτερα ὑπὸ τούτων 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 35.26.}}
**2** with _accusative_, **strip** a person of his arms, ἦ τινα συλήσων νεκύων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.343}}: generally, **pillage, plunder**, τὰ ἱρά, τοὺς θεούς, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.101, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 864d, etc.; τοὺς νεώς 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.155; θεῶν βρέτη 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 810; νεκρόν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 469d}}:—_passive_, βαρβάρων συλᾶσθ᾽ ὕπο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 600.}}
**3** with _accusative_ _of things_, **strip off**, ὄφρα τάχιστα τεύχεα συλήσειε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.466, etc.; frequently with additions, ἀπ᾽ ὤμων τεύχε᾽ ἐσύλα 6.28, etc.; τὰ μὲν ἔντε᾽ ἀπὸ χροὸς.. συλήσας 13.641.}}
**3.b** **take off** or **out**, ἐσύλα τόξον **took out** the bow [from its case], {{Refs|4.105}}; σύλα πῶμα φαρέτρης **took** the lid **off** the quiver, {{Refs|[prev. work] 116}}; with a notion of violence or suddenness, κρᾶτα σ. Μεδοίσας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 12.16.}}
**3.c** **carry off**, τοὺς πολεμίους οὐ συλήσειν αὐτά (i.e. τὰ χρήματα) **will** not **seize** them **as booty**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.36, compare 9.116}}; θεῶν γέρα σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 83, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 922, “Ph.” 1365; λόγοις τὰ τῶν προγόνων ἔργα συλήσας καὶ διασύρας 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.313; σ. κατὰ βραχὺ τὴν τῶν πυρετῶν διάθεσιν 7th c.AD: Stephanus Medicus “in Gal.” 1.295 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}:—_passive_, **to be carried off as spoil**, ἄγαλμα σεσυλημένον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.118}}; **to be taken away**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 799: _metaphorically_, συλᾶται ὕπνος ἀπὸ γλεφάρων 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus “fragment” 3.10.}}
**3.d** **exercise right of** σῦλαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 9(1).333.3 (_Locrian dialect_, 5th c.BC):}} —_passive_, πανταχοῦ συλωμένων ἡμῶν **the right of reprisals was exercised** against us everywhere, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.33.}}
**4** after {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} with _accusative_ _person_ et _genitive_ _of things_, τίς σε δαίμων συλᾷ πάτρα; **carries** thee **away from.. **, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 669:}} —_passive_, συλαθεὶς ἀγενείων **stealing from among** the boys, and enlisting among the men, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 9.89.}}

4815:
** συλλαμβάνω**,
_future_ -λήψομαι: _perfect_ -είληφα, _passive_ -είλημμαι: _aorist_ συνέλᾰβον, _infinitive_ συλλᾰβεῖν:—_middle_, _present_ in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 53.4}}: _aorist_ συνελαβόμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.49, etc.}}:—_passive_ (see. below; in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.2.14 συλληφθήσεται has been restored for συλλήψεται)}}:—**collect, gather together**, especially **rally** scattered troops, τῆς στρατιῆς τοὺς περιγενομένους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.46; τὸ στράτευμα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.3.1; τὰς δυνάμεις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 456a; σ. θοινάτορας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1217.}}
**2** **take with one, carry off**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1153; ξυλλαβὼν κατέκλινεν αὐτὸν εἰς Ἀσκληπιοῦ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 122, compare “Anthologia Graeca” 5.52 (tmesis, 3rd c.BC: Dioscorides Epigrammaticus)}}; συλλαβὼν θεσπίσματα κεῖται παρ᾽ Ἅιδῃ **with all** his prophecies, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 971; ἔρρε, τάσδε συλλαβὼν ἀράς [prev. author] “OC” 1384}}; ἔκπλει σεαυτὸν συλλαβὼν ἐκ τῆσδε γῆς **pack yourself off**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ph.” 577.}}
**3** **put together, close**, τὸ στόμα καὶ τοὺς ὀφθαλμούς (of a corpse) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 118a}}; ξ. αὐτοῦ τὸ στόμα **shut** his mouth, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 926, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 623b2}}; **enclose**, τῇ ἐπιδέσει συλλαμβάνοντα συνάγειν τοὺς μηρούς {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.86; ξ. καὶ τοὺς τένοντας τοῖσι ἐρίοισι 2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “ὀξέων νούσων θεραπευτικόν” 1.6.}}
**3.b** **combine in pronunciation**, λέγε δὴ μόλωμεν ξυνεχὲς ὡδὶ ξυλλαβών **say** αὐτὸ μόλωμεν **as one word**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 21.}}
**4** in speaking, **comprehend, comprise**, ἑνὶ ἔπεϊ πάντα συλλαβόντα εἰπεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.82; πᾶν.. συλλαβὼν εἴρηκας [prev. author] 7.16}}.; εἰς ἓν πάντα σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 234b, compare “Tht.” 147d; τὰ ἄλλα εἰς ταὐτό [prev. author] “Plt.” 263d}}:—_passive_, **to be comprehended** (logically), μετὰ τοῦ γένους αἱ συλλαμβανόμεναι διαφοραί {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1037b31, compare 998b28}}; σὺν τῇ ὕλῃ συνειλημμένος, i.e. **concrete**, opposed to **abstract**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 1039b21, compare 1035a25, +others}}
**II** **lay hands on, seize**, ὁλκάδας {{Refs 7th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.26; κόμην ἀπρὶξ ὄνυξι συλλαβὼν χερί 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 310; τέττιγα τοῦ πτεροῦ 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 143 (loosely paraphrased, compare “Hermes” 23.279); τὰ ποτήμενα συλλαβῆν 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 29.30}}; ξ. τῶν σχοινίων **lay hold** of them, **help** to pull, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 437:}} —_middle_, τίς ξυλλάβοιτ᾽ ἂν τοῦ ξύλο; {{Refs|[prev. author] “Lys.” 313, compare “Pax” 465 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II.b** **buy up**, τὰ τρύβλια {{Refs|[prev. author] “Eq.” 650.}}
**II.2** **seize the person of.., apprehend, arrest,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 12.39.7, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.121.έ, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 206, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.29, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.45, “PCair.Zen.” 15v. 44, 202.2 (3rd c.BC); συνέλαβον αὐτὸν καὶ ἀπήχθη εἰς τὸ δεσμωτήριον [prev. work] 484.21 (3rd c.BC)}}; σ. ζῶντα, ὅμηρον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 513, “Or.” 1189; τινὰ ἐπὶ θανάτῳ 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.154}}; συλλαβόντας αὐτὸν ἴσχειν **seize** and hold him, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 42(1).122.40 (Epid., 4th c.BC):}} — _passive_, πρὶν ξυλληφθῆναι before **they were arrested**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.20, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.80, +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.101, “PCair.Zen.” 15v. 7 (3rd c.BC).}}
**II.3** of the mind, **grasp the meaning of, comprehend**, τὸ χρηστήριον, τὸ ῥηθέν, τὸν λόγον, τὴν φωνήν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.63, 91, 2.49, 4.114; παρκείμενον συλλαβὼν τέρας 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.73, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 218c, etc.}}
**III** **receive at the same time, enjoy together**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32.}}
**IV** of females, **conceive**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 582a19, “GA” 727b8, 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.28, etc.; ἐν γαστρί 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 5.46; ξυλλαβοῦσα τὴν γονήν [prev. author] “Steril.” 220}}; of the womb, σ. τὸ σπέρμα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 583b29, +others, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “VH” 1.22: but συνειληφυῖα τοῦ τεκεῖν}} **near to** be delivered, {{LXX|LXX.1Ki.4.19.}}
**V** **take with** or **besides, take as an assistant**, τὴν δίκην σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 584; ἄτεγκτον σ. καρδίαν [prev. author] “HF” 833.}}
**VI** with _dative_ _person_, **take pert with** another, **assist** him, οὐ τοῖς ἀθύμοις ἡ τύχη ξ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 927, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 813, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.125, etc.}}; τὰ δυνατὰ τῇ πόλει ξ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 861}}; σ. τισί τι **take part with** or **assist** them in a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Lys.” 540 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.49, etc.; σ. τινί τινος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 946, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 734 (Lyric poetry); σ. τινί τινι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.20: with a _preposition_, συνέλαβε γὰρ ἄλλα.. ἐς τὸ πείθεσθαι}} **contributed** towards persuading, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.6, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.6.28}}: _absolutely_, **assist**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 812 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1019 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 229, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.118}}; δεῖ δὲ ξυλλαμβάνοντα τοὺς θεοὺς ἐπικαλέεσθαι while invoking the gods one must **help oneself**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Insomn.” 87.}}
**VI.2** _middle_, c._genitive_ _of things_, **take part in**, συνελάβοντο τοῦ στρατεύματος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.49; ὅστις νόσου κάμνοντι συλλάβοιτο 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 282}}; ξυνελάβοντο τοῦ τοιούτου οὐχ ἥκιστα, ὥστε.. especially **contributed** to this, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.47: with _dative_, καιρῷ χειμῶνος συλλαβέσθαι κυβερνητικήν}} **intervene** in a storm **together with** circumstance, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 709c: sometimes in tmesi, ξύμ μοι λάβεσθε τοῦ μύθου [prev. author] “Phdr.” 237a, compare “Anthologia Graeca” 9.559 (1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Crinagoras Epigrammaticus).}}

4816:
** συλλέγω**,
_aorist_ **-έλεξα**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1297, etc.}}: _perfect_ **-είλοχα**{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.308, 21.23, probably in 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus “Eup.” 2.168}}:—_middle_, _future_ **-λέξομαι**{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.292:}}_aorist_ -ελεξάμην, _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ συλλέξατο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.413}}: _perfect_, see below:—_passive_, _future_ **-λεγήσομαι**{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.100}}: _aorist_ **-ελέχθην**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.97, etc.}}, rare in _Attic dialect_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 526 (_anapaest meter_), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 784a}}; _aorist 2_ -ελέγην being preferred, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1107, “Ec.” 116, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.25, etc., and sometimes in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, 7.173, 9.27}}: _perfect_ **-είλεγμαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 294, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.94, etc. (also in middle sense, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.2.1, 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “pro Euxenippo” 32, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 59.18)}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ συνειλέχατο {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 17.10.2}}; _participle_ rarely -λελεγμένος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.26, 9.41, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 58, “PTeb.” 700.27 (2nd c.BC)}}:—**bring together, collect, gather**, [κτέατα] {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.301; χρ]ήματα 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 5.12; ἀργύριον.. τοῦ ἐπετείου οὗ αὐτοὶ ξυνελέξαμεν “IG” 12.301.32; χαλκία συνλέξαντες [prev. work]393; ξύλα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 225}}; τὰ ὀστέα, τὸν λιβανωτόν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.68, 3.107; σ. σφίσι φερνάς [prev. author] 1.93; φρύγανα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.3.11; βίον ἄνευ πόνου 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 81; ἐράνους 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.184; παρὰ τῶν φίλων ἔρανον 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.2.9; ἀμμώδη ἐν τῇ κύστει 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 5.118, compare 6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 15.12}}; σ. μονῳδίας, μέλη, **compose**, or rather **compile, scrape together**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 849, 1297, compare “Ach.” 398; ῥήματα καὶ λόγους 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.308}}; σ. ὕβρεις αὐτοῦ **compile a list of** them, {{Refs|[prev. author] 21.23: _absolutely_, ὁ μὲν γὰρ αὑτῷ συλλέγει}} **collects** for himself, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Κόλαξ” 43}}; περιιόντα συλλέγειν to go about **picking up information**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.36}}:—_middle_, **collect for oneself, for one's own use**, ὅπλα τε πάντα λάρνακ᾽ ἐς ἀργυρέην συλλέξατο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.413, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1327 (an[adot]p.), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 553c, “Lg.” 936c, etc.; ξυλλέγεται βίοτον 6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 13.50:}} —_passive_, αἷμα συλλέγεταί τινι ἐπὶ τοὺς τιτθούς **gathers** there, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 2.6.32, compare 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.55; ἁμαρτήματα.. συνειλεγμένα 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 10.1, etc.}}
**2** ἐκ τῆς ἀσθενείας σ. ἐμαυτόν **rally** myself, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Axiochus” 370e}}:—_middle_, σύλλεξαι σθένος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 850}}:—_passive_, ψυχὴν αὐτὴν εἰς αὑτὴν συλλέγεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 83a.}}
**3** _passive_, **come together, become customary**, ἡ πολυλογία σ. αὐτῷ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.3.}}
**II** of persons, **collect, get together**, στασιώτας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.59; ἐγχωρίους 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 303; χορόν 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.11; ἐκκλησίαν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.3.8}}; στρατόν **raise** or **levy** an army, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.77, etc.}}:—_middle_, ἑταίρους.. συλλέξομαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.292; λόχον -ελέξατο 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.4.8}}:—_passive_, **come together, assemble**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.81, 7.8, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 24.19, etc.}}; ἐκεῖσε σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.133}}; εἰς ταὐτὸ σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 961a.}}

4817:
** συλλογ-ίζομαι**,
_middle_, _aorist_ **-ελογισάμην**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 618d, +others; rarely -ελογίσθην [prev. work] 531d:}}_perfect_ -λελόγισμαι (see. below):—**compute, reckon up**, τὰ ἐξ Ἑλλήνων τείχεα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.148; ἕτερα σ. πρὸς τὸ κεφάλαιον 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 32.22; τὰς ἑορτὰς εἰς τὸν ἐνιαυτόν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 799a}}; ταῦτα πάντα σ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Chrm.” 160d}}; τὰ κατηγορημένα ἀπ᾽ ἀρχῆς σ. **recapitulate**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.177; τοὺς καιρούς, τὰς ὑποσχέσεις, [prev. work]47; ἐκ τῶν εἰρημένων σ. καὶ συναγαγόντας τὸ κεφάλαιον 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1042a3; μανθάνειν καὶ σ. τί ἕκαστον [prev. author] “Po.” 1448b16; τὰς χρείας 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.44.1; τὸ μέγεθος τοῦ τολμήματος 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pompeius” 60; σ. ὅτι.. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 670c.}}
**II** **conclude from premisses, infer**, τὰ συμβαίνοντα ἐκ τοῦ λόγου {{Refs|[prev. author] “Grg.” 479c, +others}}; σ. τί συμβαίνει ἐκ τῶν ὡμολογημένων{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 498e; σ. περί τινος, ὅτι.. [prev. author] “R.” 516b; σ. περὶ [τῆς μήτρας], ὡς.. διαστελλομένης 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.694; σ. ἐξ αὐτῶν ποῖός τις.. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 365a; σ. ὀρθῶς τίνος εἵνεκα ἔπραττε 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.172; τἀφανὲς διὰ τοῦ φαινομένου 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “De rerum natura - Wien. Stud.” 14.4, compare 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.40 S}}.: with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, -σάμενος τὸ ἄλειμμα οὐκ ἄξιον ἔσεσθαι {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Inscription Prien.” 112.57 (1st c.BC)}}; τὴν νόσον ἐπὶ τὴν Ἀττικὴν ἥξειν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus “Vit.Hippocr.” 7; τὸ.. αἷμα μὴ σεσῆφθαι 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 18(2).108.}}
**II.2** in the Logic of Aristotle, **infer by way of syllogism, infer syllogistically**, σ. τὸ A κατὰ τοῦ B, A of B, {{Refs|“APr.” 40b30; τὸ.. ἄκρον τῷ μέσῳ σ. [prev. work]68b16; τινὰ ἔκ τινων “Rh.” 1357a8}}; σ. ὑπάρχειν τὸ Α τῷ {{Refs|B “APo.” 79b30:}}_perfect_ in _passive_ sense, οὗτος ὁ λόγος οὐ συλλελόγισται **is** not **syllogistic, ** “APr.” 42a39; συλλελογισμένα **syllogistically concluded**, opposed to ἀσυλλόγιστα, {{Refs|“Rh.” 1357a8.}}
**II.3** συλλελογισμένον ἦν αὐτῷ μὴ πρότερον ἐγχειρεῖν ἕως.. **he had planned** not to.., {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 14.4.4.}}

4818:
** συλλῡπέω**,
**hurt** or **mortify together**, σ. τινὰς αὑτοῖς **make** them **share** their **grief**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1171b7.}}
**II** _passive_, with _future_ **-λυπηθήσομαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.39}}, also **-λυπήσομαι** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 462e}}:—**share in grief, sympathize** or **condole**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus [same place], 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.2.8; τῷ πάθεϊ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.94: with _dative_ _person_, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 1.2}}; τίς σοι -ηθήσετα; {{LXX|LXX.Isa.51.19.}}

4819:
** συμβαίνω**,
_future_ **-βήσομαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.3, etc.:}}_perfect_ -βέβηκα, _3rd.pers._ _plural_ **-βεβᾶσι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 622}}, _Ionic dialect_ _infinitive_ **-βεβάναι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.146}}: _perfect_ _infinitive_ _passive_ **-βεβάσθαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.98:}}_aorist 2_ συνέβην (see. below): _aorist 1_ _subjunctive_ _passive_ ξυμβᾰθῇ {{Refs|[prev. author] 4.30}}:— **stand with the feet together**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “κατ᾽ ἰητρεῖον” 3; διαβαίνοντες μᾶλλον ἢ συμβεβηκότες 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 1.14; συμβεβηκὼς τὼ πόδε 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 3.91}}; συμβᾶσα τὼ πόδε, opposed to περιβάδην, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Achilles Tatius Astronomus 1.1}}; Παλλάδιον τοῖς ποσὶ συμβεβηκός a statue **with closed feet**, as in early Greek art, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollodorus Damascenus Mechanicus 3.12.3.}}
**2** σ. κακοῖς **to be joined to** them, i.e. **increase** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 37.}}
**3** **meet**, σὺν δ᾽ ἔβη ἐν Φιλότητι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 21.8; τὸν συμβαίνοντά σοι 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 136 (uncertain); σ. αὐτοὶ αὑτοῖς 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.2.17}}; ξυμβέβηκε δ᾽ οὐδαμοῦ **has** never **come in my way, has had** naught **to do with me**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1007.}}
**4** **attack jointly**, ἐπὶ Ναξίους {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Parthenius Mythographus 9.1.}}
**II** most frequently _metaphorically_, **come to an agreement, come to terms**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 71, etc.}}; ἐπ᾽ ἐλάττονι σ. **agree** on (i.e. to accept) less, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“POxy.” 237 viii 11 (2nd c.AD)}}: with _dative_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.52, 4.128, etc.; πρὸς ἀλλήλους [prev. work] 61}}, etc.: with _neuter_ _adjective_, ἐὰν ξυμβῶ τί σοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 175; ἤν τι ξυμβαίνωσι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.5}}; ξ. τὰ πλείω, οὐδέν, {{Refs|[prev. author] 4.117, 5.36; τἆλλα τοῖς Λακεδαιμονίοις [prev. author] 8.98}}: with _infinitive_, συνέβησαν ἐς τὠυτὸ.., τὸν δὲ βασιλεύειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.13; ξ. ὑπήκοοι εἶναι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.117}}; ξ. ἤν τις ἁλίσκηται,.. δοῦλον εἶναι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 103; ξ. τοῖς Πλαταιεῦσι παραδοῦναι σφᾶς αὐτούς [prev. author] 2.4; ξ. πρὸς Νικίαν.. ἐπιτρέψαι [prev. author] 4.54; also συνέβησαν.. ὥστε τριηκοσίους μαχέσασθαι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.82}}; σ. εἰς τὸ μέσον **agree** to a compromise, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 337e}}; λόγοις σ., of a verbal agreement, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Med.” 737, “Andr.” 233}}: generally, **make friends with**, ἐκ πολέμου ξ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 867; ἀπὸ τοῦ ἴσου 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.19; ἐπὶ τοῖς εἰρημένοις 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 590 (troch.): in}}_perfect_ συμβεβάναι and _passive_, of the agreement, δοκέοντες πάντα συμβεβάναι that everything **had been settled**, or that they **had settled** everything, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.146; ἐπὶ τούτοις ξυμβεβάσθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.98; ἕως ἄν τι περὶ τοῦ πλέονος ξυμβαθῇ [prev. author] 4.30.}}
**II.2** **agree with, be on good terms with**, οὐ.. Ἀθηναίοισι συνέβαιν᾽ Αἰσχύλος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 807}}; σ. ἑκατέρᾳ τῶν στάσεων **hold with** one and other of them, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 2.62.}}
**II.3** of things, **tally, correspond with**, ὁ χρόνος ἐδόκεε τῇ ἡλικίῃ συμβαίνειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.116; ἐθέλων εἰδέναι εἰ [οἱ ἐκείνων λόγοι] συμβήσονται τοῖσι λόγοισι τοῖσι ἐν Μέμφι [prev. author] 2.3; ξυμβαίνει ταῦτα τοῖς πρὸ τοῦ 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 8.9; εἰς ταὐτὸ σ. τοῖς ἐμοῖς στίβοις 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 210: _absolutely_, ὅπως ἂν ἀρτίκολλα συμβαίνῃ τάδε [prev. work] 580}}; χρησμοί τε συμβαίνουσι **are in harmony** therewith, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 220, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1164}}; αὐτὸ σ. εἰς ταύτην εἶναι πέμπτην five days later exactly **tallies**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.60}}; τοῦτο σ. οὐ πλέον ἢ εἰς δώδεκα **comes** to no more than 12, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.4.12; αἱ πεντακόσιαι μάλιστά πως συνέβαινον δραχμαί 2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor “Orationes” 50(26).94}}; τὸ φαρμακεύεσθαι τῷ καθαίρεσθαι εἰς ταὐτὸν σ. **comes** to the same thing as.., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.901}}; of ashlar-work, **fit** or **range exactly**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 5.8.}}
**II.4** **fall to one's lot**, with _dative_ _person_, μοι σ. ἆται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 148 (Lyric poetry), etc.; ἡδοναί τινι 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.222; τριηραρχία μοι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 47.49; ἀτυχία [prev. author] 57.65; εὐεργεσιῶν συμβαίνειν καιρόν [prev. author] 20.121.}}
**II.5** **to be an attribute** or **characteristic of**, ξυνεβεβήκει.. Ἀθηναίοις τοῦτο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.15; τὰ ὀφείλοντα ταῖς ἀρίσταις συμβεβηκέναι τιτθαῖς 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.87, compare 91, 2.6.}}
**III** of events, **come to pass, fall out, happen**, συμβαίνει δ᾽ οὐ τὰ μέν, τὰ δ᾽ οὔ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 802}}; τῶνδε ναμέρτεια σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 173; ἐὰν μὴ θεία τις σ. τύχη 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 592a; αἱ ἀεὶ συμβαίνουσαι τύχαι [prev. author] “Criti.” 120e}}; εἰ καιρὸς σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitum magistro” 2.5; χρηστόν τι σ. παρὰ θεῶν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.11; τοὐναντίον συμβαίνειν πέφυκε 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.460: with _dative_, [prev. work]67, 16.724: also euphemistic, ἄν τι ξυμβῇ}} if anything **happen** (i.e. any evil), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.112, compare “Riv.Fil.” 60.59 (2nd c.BC)}}: generally, **occur, be found, exist**, ἐν τῇ ἀρχαίᾳ ἡμετέρᾳ φωνῇ σ. τὸ ὄνομα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 398b, compare 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 29.15: but}}
**III.b** mostly _impersonal_, sometimes with _dative_ et _infinitive_, αὐτῷ Ὀλυμπιάδα ἀνελέσθαι συνέβη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.103, compare 3.50, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.1; συμβαίνει τῷ πλοίῳ ἀργεῖν “PCair.Zen.” 650.2 (3rd c.BC),compare “PMich.Zen.” 21.3, +others (3rd c.BC)}}: sometimes with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, συνέβη Γέλωνα νικᾶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.166, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.25; συμβαίνει διὰ παντὸς ἡμᾶς περιφόβους εἶναι “PCair.Zen.” 160.6, compare 132.5 (3rd c.BC), “PEnteux.” 6.2, +others (3rd c.BC), 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.476; σ. τῷ οἰκοδόμῳ μουσικῷ εἶναι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1017a11}}; followed by ὥστε, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1152, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.79, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1261a34: with}}_participle_, σ. ὄν, γιγνόμενον, λεγόμενον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 244d, “Phlb.” 42d, “Cra.” 412a.}}
**III.c** τὸ συμβεβηκός **chance event, contingency**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Prm.” 128c; τὰ συμβαίνοντα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.43; τὰ συμβάντα [prev. author] “An.” 3.1.13; ἀπὸ τοῦ συμβαίνοντος ὁ τόπος εἴληφε τὴν προσηγορίαν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 10.28.7: hence κατὰ συμβεβηκός}} **by accident, contingently** (see. below iv. {{Refs|1)}}; τοῦ συμβαίνοντός ἐστι it **depends upon accident**, easily happens, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 4.13.}}
**III.2** joined with Adverbs or Adiectives, **turn out** in a certain way, ὀρθῶς σφι ἡ φήμη συνέβαινε ἐλθοῦσα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.101}}; κακῶς, καλῶς συμβῆναι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.63, “Cyr.” 5.4.14, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1055; τὰ μητρὸς.. ἔχθιστα συμβέβηκεν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 262}}; ταῦτα.. λαμπρὰ σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Tr.” 1174; ξυμβεβᾶσιν οἱ λόγοι.. ἀληθεῖς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 622; ἄπιστ᾽ ἀληθῆ πολλὰ σ. βροτοῖς [prev. author] “fragment” 396; σ. μέγιστον κακὸν ἡ ἀδικία 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 479c, cf. Alcaeus 1.130c, “Cra.” 398e; δοκεῖ τὸ μαντεῖον τοὐναντίον ξυμβῆναι ἢ.. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.17; τοιούτου τούτου συμβάντος [prev. author] 1.74}}; συμβαίνει καὶ σοὶ (i.e. ἄριστον) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 903d}}: _absolutely_, **turn out well**, ἢν ξυμβῇ ἡ πεῖρα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.3; εἴ μοι σ. τοῦτο 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 744a.}}
**III.3** of consequences, **come out, result, follow**, δαπανῶντες ἐς τοιαῦτα ἀφ᾽ ὦν ἡ ἀσθένεια ξυμβαίνει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.45; κάλλιστον δὴ ἔργων ὑμῖν ξυμβήσεται [prev. author] 6.33}}; τὰ συμβάντα, opposed to ἡ προαίρεσις, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.192; δηλοῦται ἐκ τοῦ συμβάντος 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 16.583; ἐὰν μὴ ὅτι τάχος ἀποσταλῇ τὰ ὑποζύγια, συμβήσεται τὰ μελίσσεια ἀπολέσθαι “PCair.Zen.” 467.8, compare 481.2, +others (3rd c.BC).}}
**III.3.b** of logical conclusions, **result, follow**, frequently in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus and 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 459b, etc.; σ. ἐκ τῶν κειμένων 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 156b38, +others, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.73}}: _impersonal_, **it follows**, with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 170c, “Phd.” 74a, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1152b25, +others; also σ. μήτε κουφότητ᾽ ἔχειν μήτε βάρος, ἔπειθ᾽ ὅτι ἀδύνατον κινηθῆναι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Caelo” 270a5: also _person_, συμβαίνει εἶναι or γίγνεσθαι}} **turns out** to be, i.e. **consequently** or **inevitably is** or **happens**, κάθαρσις εἶναι τοῦτο σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 67c, compare 80b, “Cra.” 396a, “Phlb.” 55a, 64e, “Prm.” 134b, “R.” 438e; ὅσα συμβαίνει γίγνεσθαι κακὰ καὶ ὅσα συμβήσεται [prev. author] “Plt.” 301e: hence συμβεβηκός (see. below iv. 2).}}
**IV** in Philos., τὸ συμβεβηκός has two senses:
**IV.1** **a contingent attribute** or '**accident**' (in the modern sense), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Posteriora” 73b4, “Top.” 102b4, +others}}; κατὰ συμβεβηκός '**accidentally**', opposed to καθ᾽ αὑτό, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ph.” 192b22, compare “Metaphorical” 1052a18, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Sensu” 22}}; opposed to ἁπλῶς, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Posteriora” 71b10, +others}}; opposed to φύσει, {{Refs|[prev. author] “de An.” 406a14}}; opposed to κυρίως, πρώτως, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.629, compare 16.575, +others}}; opposed to ἄντικρυς, {{Refs|[prev. author] 18(2).180.}}
**IV.2** **an attribute necessarily resulting from** the notion of a thing, but not entering into the definition thereof, οἷον τῷ τριγώνῳ τὸ δύο ὀρθὰς ἔχειν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1025a31}}; distinguished by the addition of καθ᾽ αὑτό, {{Refs|[prev. author] “APo.” 83b19, +others}}; in Epicurus, **essential attribute, property**, opposed to σύμπτωμα 'accident', τὰ τούτων συμπτώματα ἢ σ. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Ep.” 1p.6U., compare “Nat.” 4 G., +others; σ. ἀνθρώπου τὸ θνητὸν εἶναι 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ σημείων καὶ σημειώσεων” 3, +others}}; in the Stoics, **consequence**, opposed to αἴτιον, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Zeno Citieus Stoicus 1.25.}}

4820:
** συμβάλλω**,
_future_ -βᾰλῶ: _aorist_ -έβᾰλον, _infinitive_ -βᾰλεῖν: _perfect_ -βέβληκα: _aorist 1_ _passive_ -εβλήθην:—of these tenses {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus uses only}}_present_ _active_, _aorist_ _active_ and _middle_, but most commonly _Epic dialect_ _intransitive_ _aorist_ forms συμβλήτην, -βλήμεναι, _middle_ σύμβλητο, -βληντο, -βλήμενος, _subjunctive_ _2nd pers._ _singular_ -βλήεαι probably conjecture for -βλήσεαι in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.335}}, _3rd.pers._ _singular_ _contraction_ **-βληται**{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.204}}:—**throw together, dash together**, σύν ῥ᾽ ἔβαλον ῥινούς, of men in close combat, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.447, 8.61; ἀσπίδας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1405, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1274 (hexameters), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.3.19, etc.}}; **bring together, unite**, e.g. of rivers that fall into one another, ἐς μισγάγκειαν συμβάλλετον ὄβριμον ὕδωρ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.453; ῥοὰς Σιμόεις συμβάλλετον ἠδὲ Σκάμανδρος 5.774}}:— _middle_, πολλοὶ ποταμοὶ σ. τὸ σφέτερον ὕδωρ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.50 (compare δάκρυα δάκρυσι σ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 336 (Lyric poetry, _active_))}}; ὁ Ἀκεσίνης τῷ Ἰνδῷ τὸ ὕδωρ σ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 6.1.5}}; σ. τὰ ὦτα πρὸς τὴν γῆν **have** their ears **reaching** to.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 606a15:}} —_passive_, κατὰ τὰς ῥᾶγας συμβεβλημένων [τῶν δακτύλων] {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.60.}}
**2** **collect**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.1.5}}; **store up, accumulate**, κριθὰς ἵπποις συμβεβλημένας πολλάς {{Refs|[prev. author] “An.” 3.4.31.}}
**3** **jumble up together**, διαφέροντα σ. εἰς ταὐτόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 285a.}}
**4** _intransitive_ in _active_, **fit** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|compare (σύμβολον 1.1), 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Eudemia” 1239b14}}; **to be suitable**, τὰ χεδροπὰ σ. εἰς τὰς νέας {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 3.20.7}} (unless = **sow, set**).
**4.b** **to be profitable**, σ. τῷ πολιτικῷ.. δικαίῳ εἶναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.285 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus; σ. ἀναμένειν ἡμέραν μίαν 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 16.496.}}
**5** _intransitive_, **come together**, ἔνθα δίστομοι.. σ. ὁδοί where two roads **join**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 901, compare 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 6.3.7}}; τὰ συμβάλλοντα the **watersmeet,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 9(2) p.xi (Delph., 3rd-2nd c.BC)}}; [φλὲψ] σ. τῇ ἀποσχίσει {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 514a12}}; **collide**, τοὺς τύπους ἀνάγκη συμβάλλειν ἑαυτοῖς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Sensu” 52}}: Geometry texts, **meet**, τὸ σημεῖον, καθ᾽ ὃ συμβάλλουσιν the point in which (the straight lines) **meet**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ σφαίρας καὶ κυλίνδρου” 1.23, etc.}}
**6** βλέφαρα σ. ὕπνῳ **close** the eyes in sleep, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 15; σ. ὄμμα, in death, [prev. work] 1294 (but ποῖον ὄμμα συμβαλῶ}}; how shall I **meet her eyes with mine?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 455).}}
**7** generally, **join, unite**, σ. σχοινία **twist** ropes (compare συμβολεύς), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 37; so τοπεῖα “IG” 22.1672.311 (4th c.BC)}}; ὠμόλινον σ. πεντάπλουν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ συρίγγων” 4; στέφανον 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Her.Prooem.”; [αἱ φλέβες] σ. [τὸ σῶμα] εἰς ἕν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 668b24}}; **fit together**, ἁρμούς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 7.4255.23 (Oropus, 4th c.BC); σ. καὶ κολλῆσαι [prev. work] 22.1668.73 (4th c.BC)}}; κεραῖαι συμβεβλημέναι {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 566.10 (3rd c.BC)}}; δεξιὰς σ. ἀλλήλοισι **join** hands, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 58.}}
**8** σ. συμβόλαιά τινι or πρός τινα **make a contract with** a person, especially **lend** him money **on bond**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 34.1, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 425c, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.77 (_middle_)}}; συμβόλαιον εἰς τἀνδράποδα συμβεβλημένον money **lent** on the security of the slaves, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.27}}: _absolutely_, in same sense, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 21.13}}; **make a contract**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus1.125d, “OGI” 669.21 (Egypt, 1st c.AD), “Codex Justinianus [4th-6th c.AD]” 1.3.55.4}}; of a marriage contract, Mitteis {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Chr.” 372 vi 22, compare 8 (2nd c.AD)}}; **advance, lend**, πέρα μεδίμνου κριθῶν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 10.10}}; ἱμάτια, χρυσία, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 446}}; ἐπί τισι on certain terms, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 6.29; σ. δανεισμῷ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 921d}}; ὁ συμβαλών the **lender, creditor**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 56.2, compare 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 5.63 (but οἱ συμβ}}. the **borrowers, debtors**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 4.9)}}:— _middle_, with _perfect_ _passive_, **pay a share, contribute**, ὁλκάδα οἱ συμβαλέεσθαι **give** him a merchant-vessel, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.135, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 32.24, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 2.27}}; σ. χρήματά τινι εἰς τροφὴν τῶν στρατιωτῶν **advance** it, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “An.” 1.1.9, compare “IG” 7.2418 (Thebes, 4th c.BC)}}; τριήρεις εἰς κίνδυνον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.98; ἐφόδιον “PSI” 4.407.12 (3rd c.BC).}}
**9** generally, **contribute**:— _passive_, συμβάλλεταί τις.. μερίς {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 149.4:}} —in this sense mostly in _middle_, τέμενος συμβάλλεσθαι **add thereto**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 1.59; ἡ τύχη οὐδὲν ἔλασσον ξυμβάλλεται ἐς τὸ ἐπαίρειν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.45, compare 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” 2, 3rd c.BC(?): Sosipater Comicus 1.37, 4th-3rd c.BC: Damoxenus Comicus 2.11}}; τὸ μὴ ἀγανακτεῖν.. ἄλλα τέ μοι πολλὰ συμβάλλεται, καὶ.. many circumstances **contribute** to my feeling no vexation, and especially.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 36a; σ. βοήθειαν οὐ σμικρὰν πρός τι [prev. author] “Lg.” 836b}}; τιμὴν καὶ δόξαν τῇ πόλει σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator “Epistulae” 8.6; οὐ δεῖ λογίζεσθαι, πότερος πλείω συμβέβληται 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 7.13}}; frequently with μέρος as _object_, ἔργων οὐκ ἐλάχιστον μέρος σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.143; μέρος σ. πρὸς ἀρετήν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 836d, compare “R.” 331b, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 41.11; οὐκ ἐλάχιστον μέρος πρὸς εὐδαιμονίαν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 7.79; συμβαλλέσθω τὸ μέρος ἕκαστος εἰς τὸ ἀνάλωμα “PHal.” 1.108, compare 113 (3rd c.BC)}}; τὴν μεγίστην εἰς αὐτὰ μοῖραν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 47c, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.1.28: also _absolutely_, οὔτε ποταμὸς οὔτε κρήνη οὐδεμία ἐσδιδοῦσα ἐς πλῆθός οἱ συμβάλλεται}} **contributes to** its volume, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.50; σ. πρὸς τὸ λανθάνειν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.4.21, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 7.21}}; συμβαλλόμενα **contributory causes**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Sudore” 6}}: _absolutely_, **to be helpful**, πολλά ἐστι τὰ συμβαλλόμενα τοῖς βουλομένοις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.79, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 905b, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.133}}; φόνου κηκὶς ξ. **contributes** to the proof, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 1012: rarely with _genitive_ _partitive_, ξυμβάλλεται πολλὰ τοῦδε δείματος}} many things **contribute** [**their share**] **of** this fear, i.e. join in causing it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 284.}}
**10** συμβάλλεσθαι γνώμας **contribute one's** opinion to a discussion, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.61; περί τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 298c; συμβαλέσθαι περί τινος λόγους 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.2.21}}; λόγον σ. περὶ βίου **contribute** an opinion about life, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 905c}}; also συμβαλέσθαι τι **to have** something **to say**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ion” 532c, compare 533a}}; ταῦτά σοι περὶ Ἔρωτος σ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Smp.” 185c}}; συμβαλοῦ γνώμην **contribute** your opinion, help in judging, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1151}}; σ. τὴν γνώμην τῆς βουλῆς, with or without εἰς τὸν δῆμον, **communicate** it, {{Refs|“IG” 22.79.6, 103.17, +others}}; **cast** votes, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Schwyzer” 84.15 (Tylisus, 5th c.BC).}}
**II** συμβάλλειν (i.e. λόγους) **converse**, σ. τινί or πρός τινα, {{NT+1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.222c, NT.Act.4.15:—}}_middle_, ἀτὰρ τί ἐγὼ περὶ κλοπῆς σ.; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.6.14.}}
**II** **bring** men **together** in hostile sense, **pit** them against each other, **match** them, ἀμφοτέρους θεοὶ σύμβαλον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.55; ἐμὲ.. καὶ Μενέλαον συμβάλετε.. μάχεσθαι 3.70}}; σ. σκύμνον λέοντος σκύλακι κυνός **set** one **to fight with** the other, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.32}}; ἄνδρα ἀνδρὶ καὶ ἵππον ἵππῳ σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 5.1; τοὺς ἡβῶντας σ. εἰς ἔριν περὶ ἀρχῆς 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 4.2}}; ἀλεκτρυόνας σ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Smp.” 4.9; ἄνδρας φίλους [prev. author] “Cyr.” 6.1.32; εἰς χεῖρα δοῦλον δεσπότῃ μὴ συμβάλῃς 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 206: _metaphorically_, ἀναισχυντίᾳ σ. τινὰ καὶ προσγυμνάζειν}} **make** him **contend with**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 647c.}}
**II.b** _middle_, **join in fight**, σὺν δ᾽ ἐβάλοντο μάχεσθαι ἐναντίον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.377.}}
**II.c** _intransitive_, **come together**, σύμβαλον μάχεσθαι {{Refs|16.565}}; also ς. alone, **come to blows, engage**, πρίν γ᾽ ἠὲ ξυμβλήμεναι ἠὲ δαμῆναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|21.578; frequently in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, either _absolutely_, as 1.77, 82, or with _dative_ _person_, [prev. work] 80, 104}}; Ἄρης Ἄρει δυμβαλεῖ, Δίκα Δίκᾳ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 461 (Lyric poetry)}}; Ἕλληνες Μήδοις σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 136; also σ. πρός τινα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.1.20, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.69; εἰς μονομαχίαν πρός τινα 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 14.5.16}}; συμβάλλων **coming into collision**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 273a, cf. Wilcken “Chr.” 16.6 (2nd c.AD).}}
**II.2** σ. πόλεμον καὶ δηϊοτῆτα **engage in** war, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.181 (probably interpolated)}}; so in Trag., σ. βάκχαις μάχην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 837; ἔχθραν τινί [prev. author] “Med.” 44; ἔριν φίλοις [prev. work] 521: _metaphorically_, συμβαλεῖν ἔπη κακά}} **bandy** reproaches, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1323; αἰσχρὸν δέ μοι γυναιξὶ συμβάλλειν λόγους 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 830.}}
**II.3** _middle_, **fall in with** one, **meet** him, with _dative_, frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, who uses _Epic dialect_}}_aorist_ forms beginning ξυμβλη- or συμβλη- solely in this sense, Νέστορι δὲ ξύμβληντο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.27, compare 39; εἰ δ᾽ ἄρα τις.. ξύμβληται ὁδίτης 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.204; ξυμβλήμενος ἄλλος ὁδίτης 11.127}}; ὅτε κεν συμβλήσεαι (to be read -βλήεαι) αὐτῷ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.335; ξυμβλήτην ἀλλήλοιιν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.15.}}
**II.4** so in _active_, συμβαλών **having met**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 677}}; οἱ συμβάλλοντες those **who come in contact with one**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Marcellus” 20}}; φιλοσόφῳ σ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 3.9.13, compare 12, “POxy.” 1063 (2nd-3rd c.AD), “PFay.” 129.2 (3rd c.AD).}}
**III** **compare**, σμικρὰ μεγάλοισι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.10; ἑωυτόν τινι [prev. author] 3.160; ἓν πρὸς ἕν [prev. author] 4.50; τι πρός τι 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 68; πρὸς ἄλληλα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 186b; οὐδὲν ἦν τούτων.. πρὸς ἀτταγῆνα συμβαλεῖν 3rd c.BC: Phoenicides Comicus 2.5}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.10, 3.125}}; τὸ ἀργύριον τὸ Βαβυλώνιον πρὸς τὸ Εὐβοικὸν συμβαλλόμενον τάλαντον the Babyl. talent **being compared with, reduced to**, the Euboic, {{Refs|[prev. work] 95.}}
**III.b** **compare** for the purpose of checking, μέτρῳ συμβεβλημένῳ πρὸς τὸ χαλκοῦν Wilcken {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Chr.” 410.11 (3rd c.BC), etc.}}
**III.2** _middle_, **reckon, compute**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.31, 4.15, 6.63, 65}}:—_passive_, ἡ ὁδὸς ἡ ἡμερησίη ἀνὰ διηκόσια στάδια συμβέβληταί μοι {{Refs|[prev. author] 4.101.}}
**III.3** **conclude, infer, conjecture, interpret**, συμβαλεῖν τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 11.33}}; σ. ὅτι.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 412c}}; τοῦτο σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1474}}; τοῦτο σ., ὅτι.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 50}}; τὰ πρὶν οὐκ εὔγνωστα σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” [1394]; εὖ ξυνέβαλεν αὐτά 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 427; ἣν [νόσον] οὐδ᾽ ἂν εἷς γνοίη ποτ᾽ οὐδ᾽ ἂν ξυμβάλοι [prev. author] “V.” 72; σ. ἔπη 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 675; τοὖναρ [prev. author] “IT” 55; τὴν μαντείαν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 384a; τὸν χρησμόν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 532, compare 76; σήματα σ., εἰ.. ἤ.. 4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 1146: _absolutely_, καθὼς συμβάλλομεν ἐκ τοὖ.. 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.63}}:—_middle_, _absolutely_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 47, frequently in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, as 2.33, 4.87}}: with _accusative_, **make out, understand**, τὸ πρῆγμα{{Refs|[prev. work] 111; σ. τι ἔκ τινος 6.107; τῇδε, ὅτι.. from the fact that.., 3.68: with _accusative_ et}}_infinitive_, {{Refs|1.68, 2.33, 112, +others; followed by indirect question, 4.45.}}
**IV** **agree, arrange**, καθάπερ ξυνέβαλον ἢ διέθεντο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 12.46.14; πρὸς ἐμὲ πάντες συμβάλλετε 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.2.41}}:—_middle_, **make a treaty**, Foed. cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.77}}; **agree upon, fix, settle**, λόφον εἰς ὃν δέοι ἁλίζεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.3.3; ἔδει σε, καθότι συνεβάλου ἡμῖν, Ἡρακλείδην.. ἀπεσταλκέναι “PCair.Zen.” 314.1 (3rd c.BC).}}

4822:
** συμβῐβ-άζω**,
Causal of συμβαίνω, **bring together**: _passive_, **to be put together, to be knit together, framed**, ἔκ τινος {{NT|NT.Eph.4.16, NT.Col.2.19.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, **bring to terms, reconcile**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.74}}; σ. τινά τινι **reconcile** one to another, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.29}}; σ. [τινὰς] εἰς τὸ μέσον, as mediator, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 337e}}: _absolutely_, **bring about an agreement,** {{Refs|Inscriptiones Graecae 12.57.24.}}
**II** **put together, compare, examine**, τὰ λεγόμενα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Mi.” 369d}}; [τὰς μεταφοράς] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.174 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus; σ. περί τινων ὃ ἕκαστον εἴη 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 504a}} (though Timaeus here explained it _intransitive_ **agree**).
**III** **elicit a logical consequence, infer** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|compare (συμβαίνω 111.3 b), 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 155a25, “SE” 181a22; ἔκ τινων [prev. author] “Top.” 161b37; σ. ὅτι.. [prev. work] 154a36}}; σ. ὡς.. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Rh.Al.” 1426a37, 1441a6}}; σ. πότερον.. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Top.” 158b27}}; πῶς.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.172 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus (_passive_)}}; ὅτι.. {{NT|NT.Act.9.22}}: with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC(?): Ocellus Lucanus Philosophus 3.3:}} —_passive_, συμβιβασθέντος **when the conclusion has been drawn**, variant in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Sophistici Elenchi” 179a30.}}
**III.2** **teach, instruct**, συμβιβάσω ὑμᾶς ἃ ποιήσετε {{LXX|LXX.Exo.4.15}}; συμβιβάσεις τοὺς υἱούς σου{{LXX+NT|LXX.Deu.4.9, cf. LXX.Isa.40.14, NT.1Cor.2.16.}}

4823:
** συμβουλ-εύω**,
**advise, counsel**, with _dative_ _person_ et _infinitive_, **advise** one to do a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.53, 59, 2.107, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.65, etc.}}; οὐ συμβουλεύων Ερξῃ στρατεύεσθαι **advising** him not.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.46: rarely with _accusative_ et}}_infinitive_, συμβουλεύω.. συμβῆναι ὑμᾶς I **advise** that you should.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 337e, compare 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 16.501.}}
**2** without _infinitive_, σ. τινί τι {{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.71, etc.; ὅτι ἂν δύνωνται ἀγαθὸν Ἀθηναίοις “IG” 12.106.19; τοῖς ὀλίγον διαπνεομένοις ἀσιτίαν 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.508; τινὶ περί τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 319d, etc.; εὖ σ. τινί 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 38}}; σ. τι **recommend** a measure, τὰ ἄριστα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.237; χρηστόν τι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 793; πορείαν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.6.12, etc.: but with _accusative_ _cognate_, σ. συμβουλάς}} **give** advice, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 520d:}} —_passive_, συμβουλεύεταί τι **advice is given**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ep.” 330d; τὰ παρὰ τῶν θεῶν συμβουλευόμενα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.2; τὰ συμβουλευθέντα 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.13; τὰ -βεβουλευμένα}}**res de consilii sententia actae,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 7.413.58 (Oropus, 1st c.BC, Senatus consultum)}}; of persons, **to be advised**, ὑπό τινος {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“POxy.” 118.3 (3rd c.AD).}}
**3** followed by a _relative_, σ. περί τινος ὡς.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Vectigalibus” 4.30; σύμ μοι βούλευσον, ποτέρην ἄγω 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Epigrammata” 1.5.}}
**4** _absolutely_, **advise, give advice**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1370, etc.}}; ὁ συμβουλεύων or -εύσας **adviser**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1354b31, Legal cited in 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.96}}; τὰ συμβουλεύοντα τῶν ποιημάτων **didactic** poems, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 2.42.}}
**II** _middle_, **consult with** a person, i.e. **ask** his **advice**, τινι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.107, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 331a, “Thg.” 122a, etc.}}; τι in a matter, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.68}}; σ. τι μετά τινος **debate** a matter with another, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 475}}: _absolutely_, **consult, deliberate**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.1.7, etc.}} —_active_ and _middle_ opposed, συμβουλευομένου ἂν συμβουλεύσειε τὰ ἄριστα **if one asked his advice** he **would give** him the best, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.237; [τοῖς Ἕλλησι] συμβουλευομένοις συνεβούλευσε τάδε 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.1.17.}}
**II.2** = _active_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|Scholia 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 338, _falsa lectio_ in 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.5.34.}}
**II.3** **agree, make a contract**, θύρας λιθίνης ἧς συνεβουλεύσατο κόψαι {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PMich.Zen.” 37.3 (3rd c.BC).}}

4824:
** συμβούλ-ιον**, τό,
**advice, counsel**, especially with purposes of evil, {{NT|NT.Matt.12.14, NT.Mark.3.6.}}
**II** **a council** of advisors or assessors, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PTeb.” 286.15 (2nd c.AD), 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lucullus” 26}}; especially frequently of the **consilium** of a Roman magistrate, governor, etc., {{NT+2nd c.BC+|“SIG” 684.11 (Dyme, 2nd c.BC), +others, “Supp.epigram” 2.265 (Delph., 2nd c.BC), NT.Act.25.12, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Romulus” 14, 2.196e, etc.}}

4825:
** σύμβουλ-ος**, ὁ,
**adviser, counsellor**, in public or private affairs, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.24, 7.50, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1321, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.42, “IG” 22.832.16 (3rd c.BC), “PMich.Zen.” 57.6 (3rd c.BC), NT.Rom.11.34, etc.; σ. πονηρός 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.71: as _feminine_, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.1.13}}: with _genitive_ _person_, one's **adviser**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 175 (troch.), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 921, etc.: with _dative_, καί μοι γενοῦ ξ. [prev. author] “Nu.” 1481, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 8.39; μωρίᾳ ξ. τοῦ κασιγνήτου 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1019, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 2.43: also with _genitive_ _of things_, σ. λόγου τοῦδέ μοι γένεσθε 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 170 (troch.); τῆς ἀρχῆς.. ξυμβούλοισιν.. ὑμῖν χρήσωμαι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 518 (_anapaest meter_); τῶνδε σύμβουλοι πέρι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 86, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 319b; ὑπέρ τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.35: ξύμβουλός εἰμι,= συμβουλεύω}}, **advise**, with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 712, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 930e: opposed to συκοφάντης, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.189.}}
**II** as a title,
**II.1** at Athens, the θεσμοθέται were empowered to appoint σύμβουλοι (perhaps in a private capacity), {{Refs|[prev. author] 58.27.}}
**II.2** at Sparta, **a board of advisers** sent with the general, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.63.}}
**II.3** **officers** at Thurii, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1307b14.}}
**II.4** = Latin _legatus_, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.35.4}}; also used to explanation Latin _con-sul_, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 4.76.}}

4828:
** συμμαρτῠρέω**,
**bear witness with** or **in support of** another, ξυμμαρτυρῶ σοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 438, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 319 (συμ-),compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.51; ἐδείκνυε.. βίβλους αὐτῷ συμμαρτυρούσας 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.444}}; τι **to** a fact, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 36, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.3.2; σ. τὰ ῥηθέντα τοῖς ἔργοις 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.31}}; followed by a _relative_, ὡς.. σύ μοι ξυμμαρτυρῇς οἵα πέφυκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 286; σ. ὡς.. [prev. author] “IA” 1158; σ. τινὶ πάντα ὡς ἀληθῆ λέγοι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.1.35; σ. τινὶ ὅτι.. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Ma.” 282b: _absolutely_, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1224, “BGU” 86.40 (2nd c.AD).}}
**2** Astrology texts, **to be in aspect with, configurate with**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 124.}}

4829:
** συμμερ-ίζω**,
**distribute in shares**, in _middle_, πολύπουν κυσί {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 6.77}}; **parcel out**, {{Refs|Judeich “Altertümer von Hierapolis“ 336.11}}:—_passive_, τὸ πλῆθος ἦν ἑκατέροις -όμενον ταῖς γνώμαις {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 37.2.12.}}
**2** _middle_, **take share in** or **with**, κλέπτῃ variant in {{LXX+NT|LXX.Pro.29.24; τῷ θυσιαστηρίῳ NT.1Cor.9.13: so in}}_future_ _active_ συμμεριοῦσι (variant{-μετριοῦσι}) {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 264.20.}}
**3** _passive_, **to be divided together with**, with _dative_, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Inst.” 190, 5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 271.}}

4834:
** συμπαθ-έω**,
**to be sympathetically affected**, δοκεῖ ἡ ψυχὴ καὶ τὸ σῶμα συμπαθεῖν ἀλλήλοις {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physiognomonica” 808b11; ξ. κεφαλῇ τὰ μέσα 2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “χρονίων νούσων θεραπευτικόν” 1.4, compare 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 8.33}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 16.11.8.}}
**2** with _dative_ _person_, **sympathize with**, τοῖς φίλοις {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Gnomologium Vaticanum - Kleine Schriften” 66.}}
**3** with _dative_ _of things_, **sympathize in, feel for**, ἀτυχίαις {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.112 (uncertain), cf. NT.Heb.4.15, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cleomenes” 1, etc.}}
**4** _absolutely_, **feel sympathy**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. author] “Tim.” 14, “IG” 14.760 (Naples, 1st c.AD)}}; ἐκ τοῦ παθεῖν γίγνωσκε καὶ τὸ συμπαθεῖν· καὶ σοὶ γὰρ ἄλλος συμπαθήσεται παθών (_future_ _middle_ in _active_ sense) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 230, compare 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ ποιημάτων” 5.33.}}

4835:
** συμπαθ-ής**, ές,
**affected by like feelings, sympathetic**, οὐδεὶς ὁμαίμου -έστερος φίλος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 192; σ. ἐστιν ὁ ἀκροατὴς τῷ ᾄδοντι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 921a36, compare “Pol.” 1340a13}}; πρὸς τὰ γεννηθέντα συμπαθέστεραι μᾶλλον αἱ μητέρες γίνονται [τῶν τιτθῶν] {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.87, compare 88.}}
**2** **exerting mutual influence, interacting**, ἡ ψυχὴ καὶ τὸ σῶμα συμπαθῆ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physiognomonica” 808b19, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.20U.}}; νεῦρα ἀλλήλοις σ. {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 11.352 (6th c.AD: Agathias Historicus et Epigrammaticus)}}; **sensitive to influence**, τὸ ἐν τῇ καρδίᾳ θερμόν.. -έστατον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 653b6, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 1.7.4}}; of the members of an organism, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τροφῆς” 23, 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 4.5.8; ὁ κόσμος σύμπνους καὶ σ. αὐτὸς αὑτῷ 3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 2.264}}; **exciting sympathy**, χερῶν σ. ὑπτιασμός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.52 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, compare 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 2.45}}: _superlative_, {{Refs|“PHerc.” 176p.39V.}}
**3** of planets, **in concord**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 37.14; defined by 2nd-1st c.BC(?): Serapio Astrologus in “Catalogus Codicum Astrologorum” 8(4).226.}}
**II** _adverb_ -θῶς **sympathetically**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ παρρησίας” p.37 O., 1st c.BC: Cicero, M. Tullius Orator et Philosophus “Epistulae ad Atticum” 12.44.1; τῇ σελήνῃ 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 3.5.8; σ. ἔχειν πρός τινα 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 7.10.5; -έστερον ἐρᾶσθαι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Mirabilia” 846b9, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.3c}} **-έστατα** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“IG” 12(2).58b33 (Mytil., 1st c.BC).}}

4836:
** συμπαραγίγνομαι**,
**to be ready at the same time**, of crops ripening, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.199.}}
**2** **arrive** or **be present at the same time**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PSI” 5.502.24 (3rd c.BC)}}; **come together,** {{NT|NT.Luke.23.48.}}
**3** **come together with**, of planets, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 64.22.}}
**II** **stand by** another, τινι {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 59.72, variant in NT.2Tim.4.16}}; **come in to assist**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.82, 6.92.}}

4840:
** συμπάρ-ειμι**,
(εἰμί **sum) to be present also** or **at the same time**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 17, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.12, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 2.2, 12.3, etc.; frequently in Papyrus, “PSI” 5.509.8 (3rd c.BC)}}, etc.; of a desire, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.38}}; of planets, **occupy a position together**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 60.21.}}
**2** **stand by, come to help**, τινι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.6.1; ἐν ἔργοις 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “de Pietate - Herkulanische Studien” 37}}; of an advocate, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.158}}; **act as** one's **representative,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|PRyl. 120.3 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}

4841:
** συμπάσχω**,
**have the same thing happen to one**, οἱ τοὺς Χασμωμένους.. ὁρῶντες ταὐτὸν τοῦτο σ. {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Charmides” 169c, cf. NT.Rom.8.17; θαυμαστὸν.. τὸ συμπάσχειν τὰς τραγέας 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Odoribus” 62.}}
**II** with _dative_, **to be affected in common with**, ἀλλήλοις {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Priora” 70b16}}; **commotiunculis**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Cicero, M. Tullius Orator et Philosophus “Epistulae ad Atticum” 12.11}}; προσώποις, of a mimic dancer, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“IG” 14.2124.3 (Rome, 2nd-3rd c.AD)}}; τοῖς τῆς ψυχῆς παθήμασι τὸ σῶμα σ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physiognomonica” 805a6; εἰ [ὅλον τὸ σῶμα] σ. τι τῇ ἀκοῇ 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Sensu” 57}}; τοῖς ἀναπνευστικοῖς ὀργάνοις τὸ ἧπαρ σ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 18(1).25, compare 16.555, 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.20, +others}}
**III** **have a fellow-feeling, sympathize, feel sympathy**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 605d, 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 84, 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.4. Cf. συμπαθέω.}}

4842:
** συμπέμπω**,
**send** or **dispatch along with** or **at the same time**, ὕμνον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 5(4).63; ὀπάονας 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 493; πρεσβείαν “IG” 22.1.24 (5th c.BC)}}: with _dative_ _person_, νεηνίας καὶ κύνας σ. ἡμῖν {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.36; τοῖσι παισὶ φύλακον [prev. author] 8.104, compare 5.80, “PCair.Zen.” 230.4 (3rd c.BC), NT.2Cor.8.22; ἀγωγούς τινι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.12; φύλακας 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.7, compare “HG” 1.4.21 (_passive_).}}
**2** **help in conducting**, τὴν πομπήν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 6.50, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.80; συμπέμψοντες τὴν Πυθαΐδα “SIG” 697 B (Delph., 2nd c.BC).}}

4843:
** συμπερι-λαμβάνω**,
**gather together**, τὸ τοῦ ἱματίου περικεχυμένον {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus “Vit.Hippocr.” 12}}; **enclose** or **include together**, [τοῖς νεύροις] ὀστᾶ καὶ μυελόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 74d, compare 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ συρίγγων” 4; τὰ ᾠά 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 549a33; πολλὴν ἀναθυμίασιν [prev. author] “Mete.” 358a33}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 83d.}}
**2** **embrace, include**, τὰ γένη {{Refs|[prev. work] 58a}}; **comprehend** in a treaty **with** others, ἐν ταῖς συνθήκαις Philipp. cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.77 (_passive_)}}, cf. Decrees {{Refs 4th c.BC+|cited in [same work] 18.29, 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “De rerum natura - Wien. Stud.” 28.9}}; **embrace in the same** history, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 8.11.4, compare 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 16.94, etc.}}:—_passive_, ἐν τῷ λόγῳ συμπεριειλῆφθαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 142a31, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 6.1.1, +others; ὅπως -ληφθῶμεν ἐν ταῖς συνθήκαις “SIG” 591.64 (Lampsacus, 2nd c.BC).}}
**3** in literal sense, **embrace,** {{NT|NT.Acts.20.10.}}
**II** _middle_, **take part in together**, τινος {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Dom.” 4 codices}} **συμπερι-ληπτέον**, **one must include**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 6.6.1.}}

4844:
** συμπίνω** [ῑ],
_Aeolic dialect_ _imperative_ σύμπωθι {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 54}}:—**drink together**, μετά τινος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.121.δ᾽, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 277; παρά τινι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.2.28; σ. ἀλλήλοις εἰς μέθην 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Minos” 320a: _absolutely_, κᾆτά τις εἶπεν τῶν ξυμπινόντων 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 153.5 (hexameter); συμπιεῖν καὶ συμφαγεῖν “SIG” 1179.18 (Cnidus, Tab. Defix.)}}; συμπίεσθε, ἢ ο; **will you.. ?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 213a; συμπιεῖν δεινός 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.52.}}
**2** _perfect_ _participle_ _passive_ συμπεπομένον **absorbed**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Philo Mechanicus “(Belopoeica) Excerpte aus Philons Mechanik - Ath. Berl. Akad.” 61.40.}}
**3** **soak**, ἔασον συμπιεῖν ἡμέραν μίαν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.25, cf. Bilabel Ὀψαρτ.p.10, “PHolm.” 5.9, Cleopatra (?) cited in 7th c.AD: Paulus Aegineta Medicus 3.2.6.}}

4845:
** συμπληρ-όω**,
**help to fill**, σ. τοῖσι Ἀθηναίοισι τὰς νέας **help** them **in manning.. **, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.1.}}
**II** **fill up** or **completely**, ἑξήκοντα ναῦς **man** them **fully**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.50, compare “Hellenica Oxyrhynchia” 14.1; σ. τὸ περιηγηθέν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 770b}}; τὸν μεταξὺ τόπον, τὸ μ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 340a18, “PA” 694b1, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 202e, “IG” 22.1668.71; τοὺς πόρους 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Odoribus” 45, Diocl.fragment 147; ἔρανον 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.694b}}:—_middle_, σ. τὰ διαστήματα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 35c, compare 36b; τριήρεις “Hellenica Oxyrhynchia” 2.4}}:—_passive_, τὸ δὲ [τῆς σύριγγος] πάλιν ξυμπληρωθείη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ συρίγγων” 4; πάντα συμπεπλήρωται σαρξίν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 75a; εὐδαιμονίᾳ 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “D.” 1.2; σ. ἔκ τινων 5th-4th c.BC: Timaeus Locrus Philosophus 105a, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.2; ὑπό τινων 3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “πρὸς Ἐρατοσθένην ἔφοδος” 3.}}
**II.b** in Medicine texts, **cause congestion of**, τὰ ὑποθυμιάματα σ. τὴν κεφαλήν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.72}}:—_passive_, **suffer from congestion of the brain,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Inscriptiones Graecae 42(1).126.28 (Epid., 2nd c.AD), 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.902.}}
**II.2** **complete**, τὸ ὅλον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione et Corruptione” 336b31; ἕν τι τῶν συμπληρούντων τοῦτο 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 1.8.3, compare 2.6.1}}; τὰ συμπληροῦντα τὴν ἀρίστην μαῖαν the qualities **which make up the complete** good midwife, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.4, compare 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.166, “UP” 1.9:}} —_passive_, [δένδρα] συμπεπληρωμένα πᾶσι τοῖς οἰκείοις μορίοις, opposed to ἀρχόμενα φύεσθαι, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] 16.492, compare 526,685, 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 15.671a.}}
**II.3** **fulfil, attain**, τὸ τῆς φύσεως τέλος {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Polystratus Epicureus “Herc.” 346p.86V.}}:—_middle_, τὸ τῆς ψυχῆς καὶ τοῦ σώματος ἀγαθόν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 3p.62U.:}} —_passive_, {{Refs|Polystr. p.31W.}}
**II.4** **finish**, περὶ τοῦ λίαν ὀξέος ὀξυμέλιτος συμπληρώσας τὸν λόγον {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.683, compare 572.}}

4846:
** συμπνίγω** [ῑ],
**press closely**, of crowds, τινα {{NT|NT.Luke.8.42}}; σ. τὸ σπέρμα **choke** it, {{NT|NT.Mark.4.7: _metaphorically_, σ. τὸν λόγον NT.Matt.13.22, cf. NT.Luke.8.14}}:—_passive_, δένδρα συμπνιγόμενα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 6.11.6}}; οἱ ἄνθρακες συμπνίγονται **are damped down**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 96.}}
**2** in _passive_, **to be drowned**, of the Gadarene swine, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “adversus Christianos - Abh. Berl. Akad.” 49.}}

4848:
** συμπορεύομαι**,
**come, go**, or **proceed together**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.87, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1488, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.3.5, etc.; συμπεπορευμένοι τῇ βασιλίσσῃ ἕως τῶν ὁρίων “PCair.Zen.” 251.2 (3rd c.BC)}}; ἡ ψυχὴ -ευθεῖσα θεῷ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 249c; σ. ταῖς ἑταιρίαις “Abh.Berl.Akad.” 1925(5).7 (Cyrene, 3rd c.BC)}}; τῷ Χρόνῳ {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Inst.” 50}}; ἐπί τινι συμφέροντι for some advantage, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1160a9.}}
**II** **assemble**, of the Senate, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.16.4}}; of a workers' guild, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“SIG” 460.3 (Delph., 3rd c.BC)}}: _metaphorically_, **consort together, hold intercourse**, ἀλλήλοις {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lycurgus” 15.}}

4849:
** συμπόσι-ον**, τό,
**drinking-party, symposium**, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 298, 496, 6th c.BC: Phocylides Lyricus 11, 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 23.3, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 9.48, +others, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.78, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.8.10, etc.}}; σ. κατασκευάσαι, φίλοις παρασχεῖν, συνάγειν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 363c, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.198b, 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 5.186c, etc.; παιδαγωγεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 641b.-- 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus, and 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus wrote dialogues under this name.}}
**II** **the party itself, the guests**, {{LXX+1st c.AD+|LXX.3Mac.5.36, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.157d, 704d}}; ἀνακλιθῆναι.. συμπόσια συμπόσια in **groups,** {{NT|NT.Mark.6.39.}}
**III** **the room in which such parties were given**, τοῦ σ. στέγη {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC(?): Callixinus Historicus 2, compare “BGU” 1793.11 (1st c.BC)}}; σαίρειν τὸ σ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “D Deor.” 24.1, etc.}}

4851:
** συμφέρω**,
_future_ συνοίσω {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 510}}: _aorist 1_ συνήνεγκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 488}}, _Ionic dialect_ **-ήνεικα**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.152}}: _aorist 2_ συνήνεγκον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.51}}: _perfect_ συνενήνοχα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.198.}}
**A** _active_
**I** **bring together, gather, collect**, τὰ κακὰ ἐς μέσον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.152; τάλαντα ἐς τὠυτό [prev. author] 3.92, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.74}}; δαπάνην σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.99}}; especially of dead bodies, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.4.9, 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 45 codices}}
**I.2** **bring together, contribute**, βουλεύματα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 528; ἐκ πάντων γόους 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus [same place]}}; πολλοὶ πολλὰ συνενηνόχασι μέρη {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Sophistici Elenchi” 183b33}}:—_middle_, of a river, Φάσιδι σ. ῥόον {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 4.134.}}
**I.3** **bring into conflict**, πολεμίους θεούς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 510}}; **give** battle, συνοίσομεν ὀξὺν Ἄρηα {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Tyrtaeus Elegiacus 1.40 Diehl: see below 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 1.2.}}
**I.4** **bear along with** or **together**, ὁ ἵππος ὅπλον σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.3.13}}; ἐγώ σοι ξυμφέρω (i.e. τὴν παμπησίαν) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 869}}; **bring with**, λύχνον.. παῖς μοι συμφέρει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 35.8}}; of sufferings, labours, and the like, **bear jointly, help to bear**, ξυνοίσω πᾶν ὅσονπερ ἂν σθένω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 946; σ. κακά 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1366; πένθος τινί [prev. author] Alcaeus370; τὰς τούτων ἁμαρτίας 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator}}
**I.3** {{Refs|2.11: hence}}**suffer, bear with, indulge**, ὀργὰς ξυνοίσω σοι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 848.}}
**II** _intransitive_, **confer a benefit, be useful** or **profitable**, οὔ οἱ συνήνεικε τὸ ἔχθος **did** not **do** him any **good**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.37; τὸ καὶ συνήνεικε ποιησάσῃ [prev. author] 8.87; καλῶς ἂν ἡμῖν ξυμφέροι ταῦτα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 753, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 252; τοῦτο σ. τῷ βίῳ [prev. author] “Pl.” 38}}; ἅπαντ᾽ ἐπὶ τὸ βέλτιον ξ. **turn out** for the best, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ec.” 475; σῖτον.. καὶ οἶνον.. καὶ εἴ τι ἄλλο βρῶμα, οἷ᾽ ἂν ἐς πολιορκίαν ξυμφέρῃ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.26; πάντα ὅσα ἂν οἴηται συνοίσειν αὐτοῖς πρὸς τὸν βίον 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.2.5; ὃ σ. πρὸς τὴν πολιτείαν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1272a30, etc.}}
**II.2** _impersonal_, **it is of use, expedient**, mostly with _infinitive_, ξυμφέρει σωφρονεῖν ὑπὸ στένει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 520 (Lyric poetry), compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1440, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.63, etc.}}; with _Article_ prefixed to _infinitive_, τὸ περιγενέσθαι.. ἀμφοτέροις σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.4.10}}; the _infinitive_ is frequently to be supplied, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.123, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Atheniensium” 3.11; also ὡς νομίζω συμφέρειν ἡμῖν γενομένων τούτων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 230e}}; followed by a clause, σ. τῷ κοινῷ, ἢν.. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. author] “Lg.” 875a, compare “PCair.Zen.” 21.41 (3rd c.BC)}}; σ. ἐπὶ τὸ βέλτιον, ἐπὶ τὸ ἄμεινον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.8.4, Decrees cited in 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.77.}}
**II.3** _participle_ συμφέρων, ουσα, ον, **useful, expedient, fitting**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 875 (Lyric poetry), etc.}}; βίος.. ἐκεῖσε συμφέρων **profitable** even beyond the grave, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 527b; ἔστιν ἡσυχία.. συμφέρουσα τῇ πόλει 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.308.}}
**II.3.b** in _neuter_ as _substantive_, συμφέρον, οντος, τό, **use, profit, advantage**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 926, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.50, etc.; ἐς τὸ ξ. καθίστασθαί τι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.60}}; ἡδίω τοῦ συμφέροντος more pleasant **than is good for one**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 4.39}}; περαιτέρω τοῦ ὑμετέρου σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.80; τὸ σ. τινός 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 338c, 340c, +others; τὸ σ. τινί [prev. work] 341d, 342b, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.139; πρὸς τὸ σ. αὑτῷ “PCair.Zen.” 451.15 (3rd c.BC): frequently also in _plural_, τὰ σ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 131, etc.}}; τὰ μικρὰ σ., opposed to τὰ ὅλα, the petty **interests**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.28; τὰ συμφέροντα ἀνθρώποις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 875a, compare “IG” 42(1).68.84 (Epid., 4th c.BC)}}; but also τὰ τῆς πατρίδος σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.99, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 297a, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.120, etc.}}; also in _aorist_ _participle_, τό τῳ ξυνενεγκόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.51}}; συμφέρον ἐστί, = {συμφέρει}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 8, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 49, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.1.26, etc.}}; εἰ μὴ ξυμφέρον (i.e. ἐστί) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.44.}}
**III** _intransitive_, also,
**III.1** **work with, assist**, σφῷν ὅπως ἄριστα συμφέροι θεός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 627; πάντα συμφέρουσ᾽ Ἰάσονι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 13}}; συμφέροντι (or Συμφέροντι as _proper name_) Ἡρακλεῖ {{Refs|“IG” 22.2114.}}
**III.2** **agree with**, τοὐμὸν ξυνοίσειν ὄνομα τοῖς ἐμοῖς κακοῖς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 431; εἴ τι ξυνοίσεις.. τοῖς θεσφάτοις 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1233; ἐὰν μὴ τῇ γυναικὶ συμφέρῃ [prev. author] “Lys.” 166}}; **come to terms with, give way to**, τοῖς κρείσσοσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1465}}; see below {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 11.}}
**III.3** **fit, suit**, ᾗ μήτε Χλαῖνα μήτε σισύρα συμφέρει (see. Χλαῖνα) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1459; [γυνὴ] σιμὴ ἄν σοι ἰσχυρῶς συμφέροι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.4.21.}}
**III.4** of events, **happen, take place, turn out**, with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.73, 3.129, 6.23, 117, etc.}}; with ὥστε.., {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.74}}; τὰ ἄλλα.. αὐτῇ συνήνεικε ἐς εὐτυχίην γενόμενα **turned out** for her advantage, {{Refs|[prev. author] 8.88}}; see below {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 111.2.}}
**B** _passive_ συμφέρομαι: _future_ συνοίσομαι: _aorist_ _passive_ ξυνηνέχθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.44}}, _Ionic dialect_ συνηνείχθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.19, 2.111, 3.10:}}_perfect_ συνενήνεγμαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|(8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 440)}}, see {συνενείκομαι}:—**come together**, opposed to διαφέρεσθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 10, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 242e, etc.}}; **meet, associate with**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus “Epigrammata” 8.2}}; of sexual intercourse, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Herm.” 34, “Tox.” 15.}}
**B.2** in hostile sense, **meet in battle, engage**, πτόλεμόνδε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.400; μάχῃ 11.736}}; τινι with one, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 636: _absolutely_, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.36; so συνοισόμεθα πτολεμίζειν 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 358}}; σ. κακῷ **encounter** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.50.}}
**B.II** **agree with**, οὐδαμοῖσι ἄλλοισι σ. ἀνθρώπων, in custom, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.173, compare 2.80, etc.; in statement, [prev. work] 44, +others; περί τινος [prev. author] 4.13}}; opposed to διαφέρεσθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.42}}; **live on friendly terms with**, τισι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.114, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Oppianus Anazarbensis Epicus “Halieutica - Poetae Bucolici et Didactici” 5.34}}: _absolutely_, **agree together, be of one mind**, εἰ δὲ μὴ συμφεροίατο if **they could** not **agree**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.196; ἐὰν δὲ ἀνὴρ καὶ γυνὴ μηδαμῇ συμφέρωνται 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 929e; also ξ. γνώμῃ ὥστε ἀπαλλάσσεσθαι τοῦ πολέμου 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.65}}; καθ᾽ αὑτοὺς ξ. **settle their affairs** by themselves, {{Refs|[prev. author] 6.13}}; **concur**, τῇδε γὰρ ξυνοίσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 641; ἐγὼ δὲ τούτοις κατὰ τοῦτο εἶναι οὐ συμφέρομαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 317a.}}
**B.II.2** **to be in harmony with**, εὖ τοῖς πράγμασι σ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cra.” 419d}}; **adapt oneself to**, τοῖς παροῦσιν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Timoleon” 15}}; σ. τὰ πολλὰ πολλοῖς **correspond with**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 919 (Lyric poetry)}}; Χαίτης πῶς συνοίσεται πλόκο; **correspond, be like**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “El.” 527; συμφέρεται ὡυτὸς εἶναι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.79; ἔργῳ τοὔνομα συμφέρεται 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Epigrammata” 6.6.}}
**B.III** of events, **happen, turn out, occur, come to pass**, ἔμελλε τοιοῦτό σφι συνοίσεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.86; οὐδὲν γάρ σφι Χρηστὸν συνεφέρετο [prev. author] 4.157; οὐδέν οἱ μέγα ἀνάρσιον πρῆγμα συνηνείχθη [prev. author] 3.10; ἐπὶ τὸ βέλτιον τὸ πρᾶγμα.. συνοίσεται 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 594; οὐδὲ πυθέσθαι ῥᾴδιον ἦν.., ὅτῳ τρόπῳ ἕκαστα ξυνηνέχθη 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.44; ξ. θόρυβος [prev. author] 8.84; μεταβολαί 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 270c, etc.}}
**B.III.2** _impersonal_, συμφέρεται ἐπὶ τὸ ἄμεινον **it happens, falls out** for the better, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.8. ; ἄμεινον συνοίσεσθαι [prev. author] 4.15}}; αὐτῷ συνεφέρετο παλιγκότως it **turned out** ill to him,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 156; so συνηνείχθη τοιόνδε γενέσθαι πρῆγμα [prev. author] 1.19, compare 6.86.ά, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.23, +others; σ. οἱ τυφλὸν γενέσθαι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.111}}; see above {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 111.4.}}
**B.IV** literally, **to be carried along with**, ἀστράσι μήνη σ. {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Manetho Astrologus 6.319}}; κύδεα.. ψυχαῖς οὐ μάλα σ. **do** not **follow** them (beyond the grave), {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 4.4.4 (6th c.AD: Agathias Historicus et Epigrammaticus).}}
**B.V** Grammars, **to be constructed with**, αἰτιατικῇ, etc., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 285.1, +others}}: also, **agree in form with**, σ. φωνῇ [τῇ] πρὸς τὰς δοτικάς {{Refs|[prev. author] “Adv.” 209.28.}}

4852:
** σύμφημι**,
_future_ **-φήσω**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 357b, +others}}: _aorist 1_ συνέφησα {{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 342e, “Sph.” 236d, +others:}}_aorist 2_ συνέφην frequently in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus (see. below), Elean συνέφα “Schwyzer” 416.5}}:—**assent, approve**, or **agree fully**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 40, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1310, etc.; ξύμφημι κἀγώ [prev. author] “El.” 1257; ξ. δή σοι [prev. author] “Aj.” 278, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 266 (_anapaest meter_), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 72d, etc.}}
**2** with _accusative_ _of things_, **concede, agree to, grant**, ταῦτα.. πάντες ἄρα συνέφασαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Smp.” 177e, compare “Sph.” 247a, “Prt.” 330d, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.8.8, etc.: hence _absolutely_, σύμφημί}} (σοι) **I grant** you, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 403c, 608b}}; frequently in Platonic dialogue, σύμφαθι ἢ ἄπειπε **say yes** or no,{{Refs|[prev. work] 523a, compare “Grg.” 500e; συνέφη [prev. author] “Phd.” 102d, +others}}
**3** with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, **agree** that.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 553, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 831b, etc.}}; σ. τῷ νόμῳ ὅτι καλός, ={σ. τὸν νόμον καλὸν εἶναι}, {{NT|NT.Rom.7.16.}}
**4** with _infinitive_ _future_, **promise**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.2.5.}}

4854:
** σύμφῠτ-ος**, ον,
**born with one, congenital, innate**, ἀρετά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 3.14}}; κακόν, ἐπιθυμία, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 609a, “Plt.” 272e}}; of diseases, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Κωακαὶ προγνώσιες” 502; βλάβαι καὶ διαφθοραὶ τοῦ σώματος 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.3}}; **natural**, τῶν σιτίων ἔνια ἔχει γλυκύτητα σ. {{Refs|[prev. work]475, compare 731}}; σ. ἐχούσης ὑγρότητα τῆς γλώττης {{Refs|[prev. author] 16.508}}; σ. αἰών our **natural** age, i.e. our old age (_accusative_ to the {{Refs 4th c.BC+|Scholia), 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 107 (Lyric poetry)}}; νεικέων τέκτονα σ. the **natural** author of strife, i.e. a cause of strife **natural to the race**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 152 (Lyric poetry)}}; εἰς τὸ σ. according to **one's nature**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 954}}; ὕδωρ σ. ἐν γάλακτι, opposed to ἐπακτόν, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 382b12; τὸ μιμεῖσθαι σ. τοῖς ἀνθρώποις [prev. author] “Po.” 1448b5}}; σ. [πνεῦμα], i.e. the vital spirit, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Spir.” 482a8}}; σ. ὑγρὸν καὶ θερμόν (in a seed) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 1.11.1; πρῶτον ἀγαθὸν καὶ σύμφυτον ἡδονή 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 3p.63U.}}; τὰ σ. **natural functions** or **parts**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 753a17, “Ph.” 253a12.}}
**2** with _dative_, **natural to**, σ. αὐτοῖς δειλία {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 10.28}}; ἀϋδρία τισὶ τόποις σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 844b}}; τὰ ὑγρὰ σ. τοῖς ζῴοις, opposed to τὰ ὑστερογενῆ (such as milk), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 521b17, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Sensu” 1, 16.}}
**3** with _genitive_, [τῶν φθόγγων] σ. ἡδοναί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 51d; εὐβουλία ἀρετὴ λογισμοῦ σ. [prev. author] “Def.” 413c: compare συγγενής, σύγγονος.}}
**4** **like by nature, cognate, kindred**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phlb.” 16c.}}
**II** **grown together**, διάστασις τῶν σ. μερῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 145b3; σ. τῷ Χιτῶνι [prev. author] “HA” 557b18; ἐγκεφάλου σκέπασμα σ. μὲν οὐκέτι, πολλαχόθι μέντοι συμφυές 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “de Usu Partium” 8.9; σ. ἐμποιεῖν τινί τι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 81c}}; **united**, {{NT|[prev. author] “Phdr.” 246a, NT.Rom.6.5}}; of qualities in relation to matter, ὕλη.. λαβοῦσα ποιότητας.. καὶ οἷον συμφύτους αὐτὰς ἔχουσα καὶ συγκεκραμένας ἀλλήλαις {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 3.6.8, compare 3.6.11.}}
**III** **thickly wooded**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.74.6, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 40.29.}}
**III.2** **fully cultivated**, ἀμπελὼν σ. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PGrenf.” 2.28.7 (2nd c.BC), “PLips.” 1.5 (2nd c.BC).}}

4855:
** συμφύω**,
later **συμφῠτ-φύνω** {{Refs|“CR” 33.5 (near Antioch)}}:— **make to grow together, unite** a wound, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 32.11 (conjecture), 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.128; σ. τὰ ὁμογενῆ 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 378b15}}; σ. τοὺς ἄνωθεν ὀδόντας imagine them **unite into one**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “PA” 659b24}}; σ. τινὰς εἰς φιλότητα **unite** them, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 323b: but συμφῦσαι in [prev. author] “Smp.” 192e is _falsa lectio_ for{συμφυσῆσαι}.}}]
**II** _passive_, with _perfect_ _active_ συμπέφῡκα, _aorist 2_ συνέφῡν (_3rd.pers._ _singular_ _optative_ συμφύη {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.89); also συνεφύην 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1262b13, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 5.5.3, 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.36, etc.}}: _future_ συμφυήσομαι {{Refs|“Geoponica” 4.12.9}}:—**grow together**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 26.7, 95 (tmesis), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 191a, “Ti.” 76e, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.3.18, etc.}}; [ψυχὴ καὶ σῶμα] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 246d}}; of a political constitution, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.32.9.}}
**II.2** **grow together, unite**, as a wound, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 6.24, +others}}; of bones, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] “Art.” 14, 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.57}}; of the mouth of the womb and other passages, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 773a16, compare 747a12; τὰ Χείλη καὶ τὰ βλέφαρα καὶ τῶν δακτύλων τὰ μεταξὺ πολλάκις ἑλκωθέντα συνέφυ, κατὰ φύσιν δὲ ἔχοντα.. οὐ συμφύεται διὰ λειότητα Diocl.fragment 26}}; διὰ τί οἱ αἰδούμενοι τοὺς ὀφθαλμοὺς συμπεφύκασ; {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Alexander Aphrodisiensis Philosophus “Problemata - Physici et Medici Graeci Minores” 1.70.}}
**II.3** **unite with**, οὐ τῷ τυχόντι συμφύεται τὸ τυχόν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Sensu” 438b1}}; συμπεφυκυῖαι ἰδέαι εἰς ἕν, e.g. Chimaera, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 588c}}; εἰς ταὐτὸν συμφύεσθαι{{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. work] 503b; σ. πρός τι 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.924e.}}
**II.4** **become assimilated, become natural**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1147a22.}}

4856:
** συμφων-έω**,
**sound together, be in harmony** or **unison** (compare συμφωνία), ἐκ πασῶν μία ἁρμονία συμφωνεῖ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 617b, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Sensu” 85, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 919b2, Ion Eleg. 3}}; of reed-tongues, **to be of the same quality**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 4.11.7}}; κιθαρισταὶ σ. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC(?): Callixinus Historicus 2; compare συμφωνία 111:}} —_passive_, τὰ συμφωνούμενα **consonants** (literal things **which are sounded with** vowels), {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Demosthene” 43.}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **harmonize with**, ποῖα ποίοις σ. τῶν γενῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 253b, compare “Phd.” 101d, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1107a32; σ. τοῖς εἰρημένοις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 398c; τὰ ἔργα οὐ σ. τοῖς λόγοις [prev. author] “La.” 193e}}; συμφωνοῦντα τοῖς ἔργοις **in harmony with** practical experience, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 19.217; ἐπιθυμίαι οὐ σ. ἀλλήλαις 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.87; οὐ συμφωνοῦσι.. φροντίδες μακαριότητι 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.28U.; also ταῦτα πρὸς ἄλληλα σ. συμφωνίαν τὴν ἀρίστην 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1334b10}}; **hold** or **express the same opinions**, ταῦτα συμφωνοῦσι πάντες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 6.9.2; ἔντισι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 263b}}; περί τινος or τινων, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 107, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 2.47}}:—_middle_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 1.1.1}}:—_passive_, **to be agreed to**, παρὰ πᾶσι {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.20; εἰ συνεφωνεῖτο πάντα τοῖς γράψασι περὶ τροφῆς 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.454}}, compare 15.107: with _infinitive_, ἡ ἔφοδος σ. γενέσθαι {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 1.74: _impersonal_, τὴν ἁρπαγὴν γεγονέναι συμπεφώνηται 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 5.69, compare 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.391; σ. ὅτι.. 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.26.}}
**II.2** **make an agreement** or **bargain with** any one, ἰδίᾳ σ. πρὸς αὐτούς {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 302.13 (3rd c.BC); συμφωνήσας Ἡρακλείδης μετὰ Θοτέως [prev. work] 330.2 (3rd c.BC)}}; περί τινος {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.15.5}}; σ. τινὶ δηναρίου **for** a denarius, {{NT|NT.Matt.20.13}}:—_passive_, συνεφωνήθη ὑμῖν πειράσαι {{NT|NT.Act.5.9}}; ὥστε.. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 14.26}}; τὸ συμφωνηθέν **the agreement**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. author] 30.19; τὰ συμφωνηθέντα “IG” 42(1).77.20 (Epid., 2nd c.BC); τὰ εἴκοσι τάλαντα τὰ συμφωνηθέντα [prev. work]22.844.9 (3rd c.BC)}}; ἀπέχω τὴν συμπεφωνημένην αὐτοῦ τιμήν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 1643.20 (2nd c.AD).}}
**II.3** **unite for a bad purpose, conspire**, τοῖς πένησι ἐπὶ τοὺς μέσους {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1297a1.}}
**III** **agree in saying**, ὡς πάντα καλῶς κεῖται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 634e; ὅτι οὐκ ἀσφαλές ἐστι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Mirabilia” 838b34.}}
**IV** **to be satisfactory**, συμφωνεῖ μοι πάντα, ὡς πρόκειται {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“PAmh.” 2.149.22 (6th c.AD),compare “PLips.” 26.13 (4th c.AD), etc.}}
**IV.2** of remedies, **to be suitable**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Archigenes Medicus cited in 6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 9.35, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 11.806.}}

4857:
** συμφών-ησις**, εως, ἡ,
**agreement**, {{NT|NT.2Cor.6.15.}}
**II** ={συνίζησις}, {{Refs|“Anecdota Graeca - MSS. Bibl. Oxon.” 4.326.}}

4858:
** συμφων-ία**, ἡ,
**concord** or **unison of sound**, τὴν ἐν τῇ ᾠδῇ ἁρμονίαν, ἢ δὴ σ. καλεῖται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 405d; ἡ γὰρ ἁρμονία σ. ἐστί, σ. δὲ ὁμολογία τις [prev. author] “Smp.” 187b, compare “R.” 430e; λόγος ἀριθμῶν ἐν ὀξεῖ ἢ βαρεῖ 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Anthologia Graeca” 0.90a18, compare “de An.” 426a29; κρᾶσίς ἐστι λόγον ἐχόντων ἐναντίων πρὸς ἄλληλα [prev. author] “Pr.” 921a2.}}
**2** of two sounds only, **musical concord, accord**, such as the fourth, fifth, and octave, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 531a, 531c}}; ἡ διὰ πασῶν σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 921a13, compare 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης” 1.8}}; distinguished from mere ὁμοφωνία, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1263b35.}}
**3** **harmonious union of many voices** or **sounds, concert**, οἱ τῶν σ. λόγοι, the {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Pythagoras Philosophus}} doctrine **of the music of the spheres**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cael.” 290b22, compare “IG” 14.793 (from Naples).}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **harmony, agreement**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 689d, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1334b10; σ. τις καὶ ἰσότης 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 89.8; σ. τῷ λόγῳ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 401d; σ. [τῆς ψυχῆς] ἑαυτῇ [prev. author] “Ti.” 47d}}; μείξας πάντα κατὰ συμφωνίαν, of a cook, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Damoxenus Comicus 2.54}}; **unanimily**, σ. τῶν ἱστορησάντων {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.134; opposed to διαφωνία, [prev. work] 440}} **concordance**, of theory with observed fact, ἔχειν τοῖς φαινομένοις σ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 2p.36U., compare 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “de Morte - Papiri Ercolanesi” 10; also ἡ πρὸς τὰ πάθη σ. Polystr. p.15 W.: in concrete sense, ἡ σ.}} ={τὸ συμπεφωνημένον} {{Refs|compare (συμφωνέω 11.2)}}, the **amount agreed upon,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|Ostr. 364 (1st c.AD).}}
**III** **band, orchestra**, Ἑλληνικά 1.19 {{Refs 1st c.AD+|(Gytheum, 1st c.AD), “PFlor.” 74.5 (2nd c.AD), “POxy.” 1275.9 (3rd c.AD)}}, and so perhaps in {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 26.1.4, 30.26.8}}, but used of a musical instrument in {{LXX|LXX.Dan.3.5}}; so Latin _symphonia_, of a kind of drum, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|6th-7th c.AD: Isidorus Hispalensis Grammaticus “Etym.” 3.22.14, but of a wind instrument, 1st c.AD: Plinius Rerum Naturalium Scriptor “Historia Naturalis” 8.157}}; **symphoniae et cymbala strepitusque**, {{NT+1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Celsus Medicus 3.18.10; ἤκουσε συμφωνίας καὶ Χορῶν NT.Luke.15.25.}}

4859:
** σύμφων-ος**, ον,
**agreeing in sound, harmonious**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 221 (_anapaest meter_), 659 (_anapaest meter_); Χορδαί “hymnus ad Mercurium” 51; μέλος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ichn.” 319}}; **echoing to** cries, {{Refs|[prev. author] “OT” 421}}; of a musical **accompanist,** {{Refs|Anthologia Graeca 9.584.}}
**2** as musical term, **in concord** or **unison with**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 80a, “Lg.” 812d; σ. φθόγγοι 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 89.7}}; distinguished from ἀντίφωνος and ὁμόφωνος, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 918b30, 921a7}}; distinguished (as epithet of fifths, fourths, etc.) from ὁμόφωνος (of octaves, double octaves, etc.) and ἐμμελής (of smaller intervals), {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Harmonica - Opera Mathematica” 1.7}}; τὸ σ., ={συμφωνία}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 56a.}}
**3** τὰ σ. **consonants**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus 631.12, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 11.2, +others, 7th c.AD: Hephaestio Grammaticus 1.1, etc.}}
**4** **having the same speech**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 5.36.}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **harmonious, in harmony** or **proportion**, τίνες σ. ἀριθμοί, καὶ τίνες οὔ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 531c; σ. φοραί 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Anima” 406b31; ὁ βίος σ. τοῖς λόγοις πρὸς τὰ ἔργα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 188d}}; of a person, σ. ἑαυτὸν κατασκευάσαι κατὰ τὸν βίον {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 31.25.8}}; τὸ σ. **harmonious order**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Mundo” 396b8.}}
**II.2** **harmonious, agreeing, friendly**, ἡσυχία {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.70; δεξιώματα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 619}}; σ. τινί **in harmony** or **agreement with**, σ. αὐτὰ αὑτοῖς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 380c; σύμφωνα οἷς ἔλεγες [prev. author] “Grg.” 457e; σ. τῷ ὀνόματι [prev. author] “Cra.” 395e, compare 436c, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 16.790 (_comparative_)}}; ἡδοναὶ.. σ. τοῖς ὀρθοῖς λόγοις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 696c, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 6.11.14}}; especially **concordant**, of theory with observed fact, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ign.” 61; σ. τοῖς φαινομένοις 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 2p.52U., “Nat.” 11.10 (_comparative_), +others (and so _adverb_, -νως τοῖς φ. [prev. author] “Ep.” 2p.36U.)}}; rarely with πρός, as πρὸς ἀρετήν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 332d; σταθμοῖς καὶ μέτροις συμφώνοις ποτὶ τὰ δαμόσια “IG” 5(1).1390.100 (Andania, 1st c.BC)}}: with _genitive_, ὅσα τοῦ γένους ἐστὶ τούτου σύμφωνα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 11b}}; ἐγένετο πᾶσι σύμφωνον περί τινος they **were agreed**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 23.4.8}}; σ. ἐστί τινι πρός τινα {{Refs|[prev. author] 6.36.5}}: rarely of persons, σ. γενέσθαι περι τινων {{Refs|[prev. author] 18.9.5; σ. εἶναί τισι [prev. author] 30.8.7}}; of planets, **in harmony**, {{Refs|Vett. Val. 37.25.}} _adverb_ **-νως**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epinomis” 974c, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 15.18, 1st c.AD: Herodotus Medicus in “Rh.Mus.” 49.555, 58.86}}; τινι {{LXX+1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.98, cf. LXX.4Mac.14.6; σ. ἔχειν τινί 2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Geographia” 1.17.2.}}
**II.3** _passive_, **agreed upon**, σ. ὅροι {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 5.6; σύμφωνον καὶ ὁμόλογον ταῖς πόλεσιν ὑπὲρ τῆς πανηγύρεως “OGI” 444.1 (Ilium, 1st c.BC)}}; ἐκ συμφώνου {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“BGU” 917.8 (4th c.AD), “Codex Justinianus [4th-6th c.AD]” 8.10.12; κατὰ τὸ γεγονὸς σύμφωνον πρὸς Διογένην “TAM” 2.119 (from Lycia).}}
**III** σύμφωνος, ἡ, ={συμφωνιακή} 11, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “χρονίων νούσων θεραπευτικόν” 2.5}}; name of a cough-mixture used by Antonius Musa, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 13.61.}}

4860:
** συμψηφ-ίζω**,
**reckon together, count up,** {{NT|NT.Acts.19.19}}, “PMag.Leid. W.” 9.4; **reckon in, add,** {{Refs|PMag.Leid.V. 11.2.}}
**II** _middle_, **vote with**, τινι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 142, compare 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 8.15}}:—_passive_, {{LXX+2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 3.22, “Sammelb.” 7378.9 (2nd c.AD), variant in LXX.Jer.29(49).21.}}

4862:
** σύν** [ῠ],
old _Attic dialect_ **ξύν**; _Boeotian dialect_ **σούν** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IG” 7.3171.39 (Orchom. _Boeotian dialect_, 3rd c.BC): _preposition_ with _dative_ (rarely with _genitive_, σ. τῶν ἐν αὺτῷ νεκρῶν Mitteis “Chr.” 129.23 (2nd c.BC)}};
**A** σ. ἡρώων {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“IPE” 2.383 (from Phanagoria); σ. γυναικός [prev. work]301 (from Panticapaeum),compare “Ostr.” 240.5 (2nd c.AD), “PLond.” 1.113 iv 19 (6th c.AD))}}:—**with.** The form ξύν rarely occurs in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, though it is not rare in compounds even when not required by the metre, as in ξυνέαξα, ξυνοχῇσιν, ξύμβλητο, ξύμπαντ; 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus also uses ξύμπας, ξυνιέναι}}; in _Ionic dialect_ verse we find ξύν {{Refs 7th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1063 (but σύν [prev. author] 50), 6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 19.3 (perhaps old Attic), but σύν 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 4, compare ξυνωνίη, συνίημι}}; in early _Ionic dialect_ Prose (including Inscrr.,{{Refs 6th c.BC+|compare “SIG” 1.2 (Abu Simbel, 6th c.BC), 167.37 (Mylasa, 4th c.BC), etc.) ξύν}} is only found in ξυνίημι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 51, 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 95 (compare ἀξύνετος, ἀξυνεσίη, ξύνεσις), and in the phrase ξὺν νῷ (νόῳ codices) 1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 114, 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 35; 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus has only σύν, and in codices 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus ξύν has weaker authority than σύν (i p.cxxv Kuehlewein)}}; in the late Ionic of {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus, ξύν prevails over σύν}}; in _Aeolic dialect_ and _Doric dialect_ it is rare, ξυνοίκην {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 75; ξυναλίαξε 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 93}}; elsewhere _Doric dialect_ σύν, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|“Leg.Gort.” 5.6, “IG” 9(1).334.47 (_Locrian dialect_, 5th c.BC), etc.; but in old _Attic dialect_ Inscrr. ξύν is the only form up to 6th c.BC; σύν appears in 5th c.BC}} and becomes usual towards the end; after {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC ξύν survives only in the formula γνώμην δὲ ξυμβάλλεσθαι κτλ.; the phrase ξὺν νῷ is found in 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 580, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 48c, “Men.” 88b, “R.” 619b (σὺν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 909)}}; otherwise, of _Attic dialect_ Prose writers {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus alone uses the preposition ξύν, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator and Lysias have ξυν-}} a few times in compounds; codices {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus have both ξυν- (“Lg.” 930a, +others) and συν-}}; in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Sophista “Oxy.” 1364, 4th c.BC: Aristoxenus Musicus, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus, and Anonymus Rhythmicus ξυν-}} is very frequently; in Trag. both forms occur. The _preposition_ σύν gradually gave way to μετά with _genitive_, so that whereas {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus has 67 examples of σύν to 8 of μετά}} with _genitive_, the proportions in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus are 400 of μετά to 37 of σύν, in 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 346 of μετά to 15 of σύν, and in 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus 300 of μετά to 8 of σύν}}: for these and other statistics see C. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus}} T. Mommsen, **Beiträge zur Lehre von den griechischen Präpositionen** (Frankfurt 1886-95): in _Attic dialect_ Prose and Comedy texts σύν is restricted for the most part to _signification_ 8, 9 and a few phrases, such as σὺν θεῷ, σὺν (τοῖς) ὅπλοι; Xenophon uses it freely, having 556 examples to 275 of μετ; in Papyrus, {{Refs|“NT”, and later Prose its use is much less restricted (see. below).}}
**A.1** **in company with, together with**, δεῦρό ποτ᾽ ἤλυθε.. σ. Μενελάῳ {{NT+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.206; ξ. παιδὶ.. πύργῳ ἐφεστήκει 6.372; σ. τοῖσδε ὑπέκφυγον 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.286; καταφθίσθαι σ. ἐκείνῳ 2.183; ἀπελαύνειν σ. τῷ στρατῷ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.101; ἐπαιδεύετο σ. τῷ ἀδελφῷ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.9.2; σ. αὐτῷ σταυροῦσι δύο λῃστάς NT.Mark.15.27; οὐδένα ἔχω σ. ἐμοί “PSI” 10.1161.12 (4th c.AD).}}
**A.2** with collateral notion of help or aid, σ. θεῷ **with** God's **help** or **blessing**, as God wills, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.49; σ. σοί, πότνα θεά 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.391; πέμψον δέ με σ. γε θεοῖσιν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.430, compare 15.26; σ. θεῷ φυτευθεὶς ὄλβος 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 8.17}}; σ. θεῷ εἰρημένον spoken as by inspiration, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.86; σ. θεῷ δ᾽ εἰρήσεται 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 114; σ. θεῷ εἰπεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 151b, “Prt.” 317b; ξ. θεοῖς 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.86}}; so σ. δαίμονι, σ. Ἀθήνῃ καὶ Διί, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.792, 20.192; σ. Χαρίτεσσιν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 9.54, compare “P.” 9.2; ξ. τῷ θεῷ πᾶς καὶ γελᾷ κὠδύρεται 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 383}}; also θεοῦ σ. παλάμᾳ, σ. θεοῦ τύχᾳ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10(11).21, “N.” 6.24: generally, of personal cooperation, σ. σοὶ φραζέσθω}} let him consult **with** you, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.346; λοχησάμενος σ. ἑταίρῳ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.268}}; ξ. τῇ βουλῇ **in consultation with** the Council, {{Refs|“IG” 12.63.17}}; σ. τινὶ μάχεσθαι fight **at** his **side**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.3.5, compare “HG” 4.1.34}}; σ. τινὶ εἶναι or γίγνεσθαι to be **with** another, i.e.**on** his **side, of** his **party**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “An.” 3.1.21, “Smp.” 5.10}}; οἱ σ. αὐτῷ his friends, followers, {{NT|[prev. author] “An.” 1.2.15, cf. NT.Act.14.4, etc.}}
**A.3** **furnished with, endued with**, σ. μεγάλῃ ἀρετῇ ἐκτήσω ἄκοιτιν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.193; πόλιν θεοδμάτῳ σ. ἐλευθερίᾳ ἔκτισσε 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.61.}}
**A.4** of things that belong, or are attached, to a person, σ. νηΐ or σ. νηυσί, i.e. on board ship, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.389, 179, etc.; σ. νηυσὶν ἀλαπάξαι, opposed to πεζός, 9.328}} (so in Prose, σ. ναυσὶ προσπλεῖν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.2.7, etc.); σ. ἵπποισιν καὶ ὄχεσφιν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.219}}; especially of arms, μιν κατέκηε σ. ἔντεσι {{Refs|6.418; στῆ δ᾽ εὐρὰξ σ. δουρί 15.541; ἀντιβίην or ἀντίβιον σ. ἔντεσι or σ. τεύχεσι πειρηθῆναι, 5.220, 11.386; σ. ἔντεσι μάρνασθαι 13.719; σκῆπτρον, σ. τῷ ἔβη 2.47; ἄγγελος ἦλθε.. σ. ἀγγελίῃ [prev. work] 787}}; ς. (or ξ.) ὅπλοις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.2, +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 947c, 4th c.BC: Aeneas Tacticus 17.1}}; ς. (or ξ.) τοῖς ὅπλοις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.90, 4.14, “Hellenica Oxyrhynchia” 10.1, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 763a, 4th c.BC: Aeneas Tacticus 11.8; σ. ἐγχειριδίοις “Hellenica Oxyrhynchia” 10.2; ξ. ξιφιδίῳ καὶ θώρακι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.22; ξ. ἑνὶ ἱματίῳ [prev. author] 2.70}}; in some such cases ς. is little more than expletive, as σ. τεύχεσι θωρηχθέντες _lines_{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8.530, etc.: with αὐτός compare (αὐτός 1.5), chiefly in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, ἀνόρουσεν αὐτῇ σ. φόρμιγγι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.194, compare 14.498; αὐτῷ σ. τε λίνῳ καὶ ῥήγεϊ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.118.}}
**A.5** of things accompanying, or of concurrent circumstances, ἄνεμος σ. λαίλαπι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.57, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.408}}; of coincidence in time, ἄκρᾳ σ. ἑσπέρᾳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 11.10}}; καιρῷ σ. ἀτρεκεῖ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 8.7; σ. τῷ Χρόνῳ προϊόντι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.7.6}}; **in the course of**, κείνῳ σ. ἄματι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 10.23, compare 125, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 123.}}
**A.6** of necessary connexion or consequence, σὺν μεγάλῳ ἀποτεῖσαι to pay **with** a great loss, i.e. suffer greatly, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.161}}; δημοσίῳ σ. κακῷ **with** loss to the public, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 50}}; σ. τῷ σῷ ἀγαθῷ **to** your advantage, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.1.15}}; ὤλοντο.. σὺν μιάς ματι **with** pollution, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 172}}; to denote agreement, **in accordance with**, σ. τῷ δικαίῳ καὶ καλῷ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.6.18; σ. δίκᾳ 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 9.96; σ. κόσμῳ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.86, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Mundo” 398b23; σ. τάχει 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 872, etc.}}
**A.7** of the instrument or means, **with the help of, by means of**, σ. ἐλαίῳ φαρμακώσαισα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.221; διήλλαχθε σ. σιδάρῳ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 885 (Lyric poetry); πλοῦτον ἐκτήσω ξ. αἰχμῇ [prev. author] “Pers.” 755 (troch.); ἡ [τῶν φίλων] κτῆσίς ἐστιν οὐδαμῶς σ. τῇ βίᾳ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.7.13; ξ. ἐπαίνῳ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.84.}}
**A.8** **including**, κεφάλαιον σ. ἐπωνίοις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 12.329.5, compare 22.1388.85, 1407.12, +others; τοῦ Πειραιῶς ξ. Μουνυχίᾳ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.13, compare 4.124, 5.26, 74, 7.42, 8.90, 95}}; δισχίλιαι γάρ εἰσι (i.e. δραχμαὶ) σ. ταῖς Νικίου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 100; ἀνήλωσα σ. τῇ τῆς σκευῆς ἀναθέσει ἑκκαίδεκα μνᾶς 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 21.4, compare 2; αἶγας ἀπέδοτο σ. τῷ αἰπόλῳ τριῶν καὶ δέκα μνῶν 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 6.33, compare 8.8, 35, 11.42, 46, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.162, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.155, 27.23, +others, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 525b15, 17, “Ath.” 19.6, 2nd c.BC: Hipparchus Astronomicus 1.1.9, +others, “PSI” 10.1124.14 (2nd c.AD).}}
**A.9** **excluding, apart from, plus**, ἓξ ἐμοὶ σ. ἑβδόμῳ six **with** (but not including) me the seventh, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 283; αἱ γὰρ καμπαὶ τέτταρες, ἢ δύο σ. τοῖς πτερυγίοις 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 490a32; σ. τοῖς ἀρχαίοις τὸν οἶκον ἐκ τῶν προσόδων μείζω ποιῆσαι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.61; τὴν ἐφαπτίδα σ. τῇ σακκοπήρᾳ ἐν ᾗ ἐνῆν}}**together with..,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|PEnteux. 32.7, compare 89.9 (3rd c.BC)}}; οἱ γραμματεῖς σ. τοῖς πρεσβυτέροις {{NT|NT.Luke.20.1, cf. NT.Gal.5.24.}}
**B** POSITION:—σύν sometimes follows its case, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.19, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.332, 15.410}}. It frequently stands between _adjective_ and _substantive_, as {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.359, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.194, etc.}}; more rarely between _substantive_ and _adjective_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.258, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 8.7.}}
**B.2** frequently in tmesis in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, as 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.687, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.296, etc.}}
**B.3** in late Gr. σὺν καὶ with _dative_, στεφανηφορήσας σ. καὶ Αὐρ. Ἰάσονι {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“IG” 12(7).259 (Amorgos, 3rd c.AD),compare “Supp.epigram” 4.535 (Ephesus, 2nd-3rd c.AD), “Rev.Phil.” 50.11 (Sardis, 1st-3rd c.AD), “CPR” 26.16 (2nd c.AD); compare below with 2.}}
**C** σύν AS _adverb_., **together, at once**, κενεὰς σ. Χεῖρας ἔχοντες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.42}}; mostly followed by δέ or τε, σ. δὲ πτερὰ πυκνὰ λίασθεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.879; σ. τε δύ᾽ ἐρχομένω 10.224 (compare σύνδυο); ξ. τε διπλοῖ βασιλῆς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 960 (Lyric poetry)}}. It is sometimes hard to distinguish this from tmesis, e.g. in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.879}}; so ξὺν κακῶς ποιεῖν is ={Ξυγκακοποιεῖν} in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.13}}. In Old Testament Gr. it is sometimes used to translate the Hebrew **'ēth** (particle prefixed to the definite _accusative_) through confusion with the _preposition_ **'ēth** 'with', ἐμίσησα σὺν τὴν ζωήν {{LXX|LXX.Eccl.2.17}}; οὐκ ἐμνήσθη σ. τοῦ ἀνδρός{{LXX+2nd c.AD+|[prev. work] 9.15; ἔκτισεν ὁ θεὸς σὺν τὸν οὐρανὸν καὶ σὺν τὴν γῆν 2nd c.AD: Aquila LXX.Gen.1.1, etc.}}
**C.2** **besides, also**, σ. δὲ πλουτίζειν ἐμέ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 586; σ. δ᾽ αὔτως ἐγώ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 85, etc.; σ. δ᾽ ἐγὼ παρών [prev. author] “Aj.” 1288, compare “El.” 299; Δίρκα τε.. σ. τ᾽ Ἀσωπιάδες κόραι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 785 (Lyric poetry)}}; in later Poetry σ. καί {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 1.74, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 4.3, 2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Theriaca” 8, 2nd c.AD: Dionysius Periegeta 843}} (also in late Prose, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 2.49a; compare above 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 3).}}
**D** IN COMPOSITION (joined with other words),
**D.I** **with, along with, together, at the same time**, hence of any kind of union, connexion, or participation in a thing, and _metaphorically_ of agreement or unity. In Compos. with a _transitive_ Verb σύν may refer to the Object as well as the Subject, as συγκατακτείνειν may mean **kill one person as well as another**, or, **join with another in killing.**
**D.I.2** of the completion of an action, **altogether, completely**, as in συνάγνυμι, συνασκέω 2, συνθρύπτω, συγκόπτω, συμπατέω, συμπληρόω, συντελέω, συντέμν; sometimes, therefore, it seems only to strengthen the force of the simple word.
**D.I.3** joined with numerals, σύνδυο **two together**, which sense often becomes distributive, **by twos, two and two**; so σύντρεις, σύμπεντε, etc., like Latin _bini_, **terni**, etc.
**D.II** σύν in Compos., before β μ π φ ψ, becomes συμ-; before γ κ ξ Χ, συγ-; before λ συλ-; before σ usually συσ-; but becomes συ- before σ followed by a _consonant_ (e.g. συστῆναι), before ζ, and perhaps sometimes before ξ. In a _poetry_ passage, cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 237a, we have ξύμ alone in tmesi, ξύμ μοι λάβεσθε for συλλάβεσθέ μο;compare ὅτε ξὺμ πρῶτ᾽ ἐφύοντο 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 95: in Inscrr. and Papyri these assimilations are frequently not found.}}

4863:
** συνάγω** [ᾰ],
_imperfect_ συνῆγον, _Doric dialect_ **-ᾶγον**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 756 (Lyric poetry), probably in 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 290 (Lyric poetry)}}, _Epic dialect_ σύνᾰγον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.448:}}_future_ συνάξω: _aorist 1_ συνῆξα, _Doric dialect_ **-ᾶξα**{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“GDI” 1772,1791 (Delph., 2nd c.BC)}}; _infinitive_ συνάξαι variant in {{NT|NT.Luke.3.17}}; _participle_ συνάξας _falsa lectio_ for{συννάξας} in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.60: but the regular}}_aorist_ is συνήγαγον: _Attic dialect_ _perfect_ συνῆχα [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.2.8; συναγήοχα 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Oeconomica” 1346a28 (variant{-γιοχ-, -γιωχ-, γειοχ-}), 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.68, 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de vita Pythagorica” 35.254, etc.}}]; _Doric dialect_ συναγάγοχα {{Refs|“Test.Epict.” 3.12:}}_perfect_ _passive_ συνῆγμαι, _Doric dialect_ **-ᾶγμαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-4th c.BC: Timaeus Locrus Philosophus 101b}}.--Old _Attic dialect_ **ξυνάγω**, which {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} also uses for the sake of meter:—**bring together, gather together**:
**I** of persons, animals, etc., ἡ δὲ ξυνάγουσα γεραιὰς νηόν.. **to** the temple, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.87, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.111, 3.150, etc.; ἐς ἕνα Χῶρον σ. μυριάδα ἀνθρώπων [prev. author] 7.60; ἔνθα ποτ᾽ Ὀρφεὺς σύναγεν δένδρεα μούσαις, σύναγεν θῆρας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 563 (Lyric poetry)}}; ποίμνας Ὀλύμπου σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 522}}; Ἕλληνας εἰς ἓν καὶ Φρύγας σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1640, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 585 (_anapaest meter_)}}; σ. ἐς ὀλίγον **crowd** them into a narrow compass, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.84; σ. εἰς ταὐτόν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 256c, compare “Tht.” 194b}}; εἰς ἕν, εἰς μίαν ἀρχήν, [{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1280b13, 1299b13; much like{συνοικίζω}, [prev. work] 1285b7.}}]
**I.2** **bring together** for deliberation or festivity, βουλήν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Batrachomyomachia 134; δικαστήριον 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.85; τοὺς στρατηγούς [prev. author] 8.59; ἐκκλησίαν τινὸς ἕνεκα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.60}}; ἔς τι, περί τινος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.120, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.1.27; οἱ νόμοι σ. ὑμᾶς, ἵνα.. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.1; τὴν βουλὴν καὶ τὸν δῆμον 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 43.3}}; σ. πανηγύρεις, ἑταιρείας, συσσίτια, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.1,79, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 365d, “Lg.” 625e, etc.; σ. ἔρανον Μηνὶ Τυράννῳ “IG” 3.74.21, compare “GDI” 1772, 1791 (Delph., 2nd c.BC)}}:—_passive_, πανήγυρις.. συναγομένη {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“SIG” 888.129 (Scaptopara, 3rd c.AD)}}: _absolutely_, **hold a club dinner** or **meeting**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 30.18, and so perhaps “OGI” 130.5 (Egypt, 2nd c.BC)}}; σ. ἀπὸ συμβολῶν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 43.28; ἔλεγον συνάγειν τὸ μετ᾽ ἀλλήλων πίνειν 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 8.365c, compare 4th c.BC(?): Sophilus Comicus 4.2, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 158, 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}; νυνὶ.. συνάγουσι they **are at dinner**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 195.}}
**I.3** in hostile sense, ξ. Ἄρηα, ἔριδα Ἄρηος, ὑσμίνην, **join** battle, **begin** the battle-strife, etc., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.381, 5.861, 14.448, +others}}; πόλεμον σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.84.}}
**I.3.b** **match, pit** two warriors **one against the other**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 508: hence _intransitive_, ἐς μέσσον σ}}. **engage** in fight, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 22.82; σ. τινί 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 11.18.4; εἰς Χεῖρας 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Publicola” 9.}}
**I.3.c** **collect** or **levy** soldiers, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.1.5, etc.}}; **collect** slaves for work, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PMich.Zen.” 62.15 (3rd c.BC).}}
**I.4** **bring together, join in one, unite**, ἄμφω ἐς φιλότητα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“hymnus ad Mercurium” 507; παράνοια σ. νυμφίους φρενώλεις 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 756 (Lyric poetry)}}; τὸ κακὸν σέ τε κἀμὲ σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 644 (Lyric poetry), compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 991 (Lyric poetry); ἀνθρώπους εἰς κηδείαν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.6.36}}; γυναῖκα καὶ ἄνδρα, of Isis, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“IG” 12(5).14.20 (Ios, 3rd c.AD): hence γάμους σ}}. **contract** marriages, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 4.64.}}
**I.5** **bring together, make friends of, reconcile**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus cited in 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1000b11, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 58.42, 59.45}}; **bring** persons **together** in works of fiction, Κρέοντα καὶ Τειρεσίαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 311b.}}
**I.6** σ. ἑαυτόν **collect** oneself, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Philopoemen” 20.}}
**I.7** **lead with one, receive**, σ. εἰς τὸν οἶκον {{LXX|LXX.2Ki.11.27, cf. LXX.Judg.19.15; ξένος ἤμην καὶ συνηγάγετέ με}}**gave hospitality to..,** {{NT|NT.Matt.25.35}}:—_passive_, {{NT|NT.Act.11.26.}}
**II** of things, σύναγεν νεφέλας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.291, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Ventis” 42; ἵνα οἱ σὺν φόρτον ἄγοιμι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.296; κήρυκες ὅρκια πιστὰ θεῶν σύναγον 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.269; τὰ Χρήματα ἐκ τῶν ἀγρῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.2.8}}; τὸ ἔλαιον ἐν ἀγγείοις interpolated in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.119; τὰς εἰσφοράς 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1314b15, compare “PHib.” 1.157 (3rd c.BC), “PCair.Zen.” 315.1 (3rd c.BC), etc.}}; καρπόν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 12.2.5; κόγχον καὶ κύαμον 4th c.BC: Crates Thebanus Poeta Philosophus 7}}; τρυγᾶν καὶ σ. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PRev.Laws” 24.14 (3rd c.BC)}}; τὴν μήκωνα σ. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Sammelb.” 4305 (3rd c.BC)}}; σ. εἰς μίαν γωνίαν τὸ ἀποκτένισμα τοῦ στιππύου {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 176.41 (3rd c.BC)}}; συναγαγεῖν καὶ συναθροῖσαι τὸ θερμόν {{NT+5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Igne” 17; εἰς ἀποθήκας NT.Matt.6.26; κοινὸν σ. τὸν βίον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 311c; σ. ἐκ δικαίων τὸν βίον 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Μονόστιχοι - Fragmenta Comicorum Graecorum” 196}}; of an artist, σ. τὰ κάλλιστα ἐκ πολλῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.10.2, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 488a.}}
**II.b** of a historical writer, σ. τὰς πράξεις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.252, 15.45}}; συνηγμένος **concise** in speech, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 4.33}}; of an anthologist, ὅλας ῥήσεις εἰς ταὐτὸν σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 811a}}; σ. εἰς ταὐτὸν τὰ κάλλιστα τοῖς αἰσχίστοις **jumble together, identify**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.145, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 251d; Σειληνὸν καὶ Μαρσύαν.. εἰς ἕν 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 10.3.14.}}
**II.2** **draw together**, so as to make the extremities meet, τὰ κέρεα (of an army) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.113}}; Αἴας δὲ.. δεξιὸν κέρας πρὸς τὸ λαιὸν (uncertain reading) ξυνᾶγε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 290 (Lyric poetry); σ. ἐς τετράγωνον τάξιν τοὺς ὁπλίτας 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.125, compare 1.63, etc.}}; σ. τὰ τέρματα, of two rivers which gradually approach one another, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.52}}; σ. ἑαυτόν, of a snake, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 594a19}}; σ. τοὺς πόρους, of a styptic, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Odoribus” 36}}; σ. τὰν ἁφάν, τὰν γεῦσιν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-4th c.BC: Timaeus Locrus Philosophus 101c; συναγμένα [φωνά] [prev. work] 101b.}}
**II.2.b** **draw together, narrow, contract**, [τὴν διώρυχα] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.23}}; πρῴρην σ. **bring** it **to a point**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.194}}; τὸν.. Χρόνον ὡς εἰς μικρότατον σ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “Prooemia” 36; τὴν πόλιν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.93.5, etc.; ἐκ μεγάλας δαπάνας εἰς μικρόν “IG” 12(2).645 a. 16 (Nesos, 4th c.BC)}}:—_passive_, συνάγεται καὶ διοίγεται ὁ φάρυγξ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 664b25; εἰς ὀξὺ συνῆχθαι [prev. author] “HA” 496a19; εἰς μικρόν [prev. author] “Mete.” 354a7, 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus cited in 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Igne” 52}}; εἰς στενόν Didym. cited in {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 11.477f; ποτήριον συνηγμένον εἰς μέσον 3rd c.BC(?): Callixinus Historicus 3}}; συνῆκται ἡ κοιλία **is pinched in, drawn in**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Archigenes Medicus cited in 6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 6.3; ἐπὶ στενὸν συνάγεται τὸ στόμιον 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.9.}}
**II.2.c** σ. τὰς ὀφρῦς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 1121, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 582 (troch.), 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 218.2; ἐπισκύνιον 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 823 (Lyric poetry); τὰ ὄμματα 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 958a21}}; σ. τὰ βλέφαρα **close** the eyelids,{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. work] 38, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 18(2).62}}; but σ. τὰ ὦτα **prick** the ears, of dogs, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 3.5, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1348; τὰ σκέλη πρὸς ἄλληλα 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.101, compare 2.61 (_passive_), Diocl.fragment 141.}}
**II.2.d** _metaphorically_, σ. τινὰς ἐς κίνδυνον ἔσχατον {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Ἀννιβαϊκή” 60}}; συνάγεσθαι **to be straitened, afflicted**, λιμῷ, σιτοδείᾳ, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.18.7, 10}}; συνάγεσθαι τοῖς Χαρακτῆρσι **to become pinched** in its features, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.108}}; but πεφυκότος τοῦ θερμοῦ συνάγειν καὶ τονοῦν τὴν γαστέρα **pull** the stomach **together**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.195; τὰ στύφοντα ἐδέσματα σ. καὶ σφίγγει τὰ σώματα [prev. work]462, compare 6.90, +others}}
**II.3** **conclude** from premisses, **infer, prove**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1357a8, 1395b25, “Metaphorical” 1042a3, “Pol.” 1299b12, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ σημείων καὶ σημειώσεων” 12, +others; σ. ὅτι.. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1377b6, compare 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Conjunctionibus;” 249.7}}: with _infinitive_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Hist.Conscr.” 16: with _genitive_ _absolutely_, σ. ὥς τινος γενομένου}} **form a conclusion of** his having been.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1274a25}}; συνάγοντες λόγοι **cogent** arguments, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta” 2.77, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 1.7.12}}: also, **sum up** numbers, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 4.6, 1st c.BC: Ptolemaeus Epigrammaticus “Alm.” 9.10, 3rd c.AD: Diophantus Mathematicus 3.6, +others}}; also, **obtain** them by multiplication, ὁ συνηγμένος [ἀριθμὸς] ἐκ τῶν κβ καὶ πθ the **product.. **, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Aristarchus Samius Astronomus 13, compare 3rd c.AD(?): Pappus Mathematicus 22.7, 4th c.AD: Paulus Alexandrinus Astrologus “K.” 1}}; of division, **give** a quotient, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Diophantus Mathematicus 2.9}}; of an integer, **yield** a fraction {{Refs|(9 = 72 / 8), [prev. work] 12}}; of any calculation, **yield** a result, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.25, +others (_passive_).}}
**II.4** _passive_, συνάγεται τᾷ περιφορᾷ **is carried along with** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-4th c.BC: Timaeus Locrus Philosophus 98e.}}
**II.5** **bring about**, τὸ τέλος τῆς νίκης {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 1.101; also σ. τι εἰς τέλος [prev. work] 5.145.}}

4864:
** συνᾰγωγ-ή**, ἡ,
**a bringing together**:
**I** of persons, ἀνδρὸς καὶ γυναικός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 150a}}; **collecting**, ὄχλων, ἀνδρῶν, etc., {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.7.6, 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 2.129, etc.; συμποσίου 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 5.192b}}; **assembling, meeting**, τῶν λογιστῶν {{Refs|“IG” 12.91.9, compare “Test.Epict.” 4.7.}}
**I.2** **assembly**, {{LXX+2nd c.BC+|LXX.Exo.12.3, “OGI” 737.1 (Egypt, 2nd c.BC), etc.}}; τῶν συνέδρων {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“IG” 5(1).1390.49 (Andania, 1st c.BC),compare “Test.Epict.” 4.25}}; **place of assembly**, especially of the Jewish **synagogue,** {{NT|NT.Luke.8.41, NT.Act.9.2, “BCH” 56.293 (from Stobi), etc.}}; **meeting-house**, Μαρκιωνιστῶν {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“OGI” 608.1 (Syria, 4th c.AD)}}; **conventicle,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-6th c.AD: Codex Justinianus 1.5.18.3.}}
**II** of things, σ. [τῶν ἐκπεπταμένων] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “κατ᾽ ἰητρεῖον” 11, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “De rerum natura - Wien. Stud.” 14.4, etc.}}; opposed to διαιρέσεις, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 266b}}; σ. πολέμου **levying** of war, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.18}}; **gathering in** of harvest, τοῦ σίτου {{LXX+3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 433.5 (3rd c.BC), 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.17.9, etc.; Χρημάτων 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 222, “SIG” 410.14 (Erythrae, 3rd c.BC), 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 27.12.2, compare 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ οἰκονομίας” p.51 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus; ὑδάτων LXX.Gen.1.9 (_plural_), cf. LXX.Lev.11.36}}; πύου Heras cited in {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 13.815 (_plural_); ξύλων “PMich.Zen.” 84.15 (3rd c.BC)}}; **harvest**, ἑορτὴ συναγωγῆς {{LXX|LXX.Exo.34.22.}}
**II.2** **drawing together, contracting**, συναγωγὰς καὶ ἐκτάσεις στρατιᾶς **forming** an army **in column** or in line, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 526d}}; **contraction** of ranks either in front or depth, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Tactica” 11.3}}; αἱ τοῦ προσώπου σ. **pursing up** or **wrinkling** of the face, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.44; μετώπου 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Κωακαὶ προγνώσιες” 210}}; **bringing together, closing up** of a wound, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 10.191; σ. τῶν μηρῶν 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.41; τῶν ὀφθαλμῶν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 876b10}}; opposed to διαστολή, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ph.” 217b15}}; σ. ἔχειν, σ. λαμβάνειν, ={συνάγεσθαι}, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 3.10.5, “PCair.Zen.” 54.6 (3rd c.BC), 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 8.2.3, compare 12.2.4.}}
**II.3** **collection**, τῶν νόμων καὶ τῶν πολιτειῶν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1181b7 (_plural_)}}; of writings, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 2.27, 1st c.BC: Cicero, M. Tullius Orator et Philosophus “Epistulae ad Atticum” 9.13.3, 16.5.5, 1st c.AD: Herodotus Medicus in “Rh.Mus.” 58.114, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 12.836, 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 1 “Prooem.” 2.}}
**II.4** **combination**, [πολιτειῶν] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1316b40.}}
**II.5** **conclusion, inference**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] “Rh.” 1400b26, 1410a22, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 16.676, 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 2.143, 170}}; **cogent reasoning**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 2.89}}; **demonstration**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.91 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}

4865:
** συνᾰγων-ίζομαι**,
_Doric dialect_ _aorist_ συναγωνιξάμην {{Refs|“Delph.” 3(3).126, etc.}}:— **contend along with, share in a contest**, τινι **with** one, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 1061, compare 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.93, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.143, etc.; τινὶ πρὸς τοὺς πολεμίους 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus1.119e; ἀλλήλοις ἐφ᾽ ἡμᾶς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 43.10; τινὶ ἐν ταῖς προσευχαῖς NT.Rom.15.30; σ. ἐν μάχῃ “Marm.Par.” 63: c._accusative_ _cognate_, ἀγῶνα “SIG” 711 L 29 (Delph., 2nd c.BC)}}; μάχας {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“OGI” 280.3 (Pergam., 3rd c.BC).}}
**b** generally, ξ. τινί **share in the fortunes of** another, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.64.}}
**2** **aid, succour**, τινι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.190}}; τινί τι one in a thing, {{Refs|[prev. author] 18.25, 30.31}}; τινὶ πρός τι one towards a thing, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 18.20; εἴς τι 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 4.4, “Michel” 452 (4th-3rd c.BC)}}: generally, **assist**, τῇ διατροπῇ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.AD: Metrodorus Epigrammaticus “Herc.” 831.19; μετὰ σοῦ κοινῇ 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 48.43.}}
**3** _absolutely_, **fight on the same side**, οἱ ξυναγωνιούμενοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.109, compare 1.123, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.5.49, etc.}}; of the Tragic chorus, **join in the action**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1456a26:}} —_active_ -ίζω, uncertain reading in {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Nicolaus Damascenus Historicus “fragment” 130.18 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus}}

4866:
** συναθλέω**,
={συναγωνίζομαι},{τινι} **with** one, {{NT|NT.Phil.4.3}}; **struggle together**, τινι for a thing, {{Refs|[prev. work] 1.27.}}
**II** **impress by practice upon**, μεταφορᾶς μνήμῃ συνηθλημένης {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 3.4.}}

4867:
** συναθρ-οίζω**,
_perfect_ -ήθροικα (-υκα Papyrus) {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“POxy.” 1253.5 (4th c.AD)}}:— **gather together, assemble**, especially of soldiers, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.2.8, etc.; τὸ ναυτικόν 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.34; ἀγέλην 2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 124.8; σ. ἐπὶ τὴν πόλιν.. Ἕλληνάς τε καὶ βαρβάρους 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 243b}}:—_passive_, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.5.30, NT.Act.12.12.}}
**2** of things, **gather into one mass**, τὸ κάταγμα εἰς ἕν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 585 (_anapaest meter_)}}; τὸ σῶμα σ. **bring** the body **together**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 44d}}:—_passive_, ἐὰν εἰς μίαν.. πόλιν.. συναθροισθῇ τὰ.. Χρήματα {{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 422d}}; τούτων συνηθροισμένων **to sum up**, therefore,{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 563d; σ. εἰς ἕν [prev. author] “Ti.” 25b; εἰς ταὐτό 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 546b18; συνηθροισμένη τῇ πόλει δόξα 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 110; συνηθροισμένον πᾶν ἰσχυρόν 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Igne” 12, compare “Vent.” 26; Χρόνῳ τὸ μῖσος -ηθροίσθη 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “de Pietate - Herkulanische Studien” 30.}}
**3** of a single person, οὐ συνήθροισται στρατῷ **has** not **joined** the main army, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 613.}}

4868:
** συναίρω**,
_poetry_ **συναείρω** (which see):—_middle_ _aorist_ συνηράμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 767, etc.}}; _poetry_ _aorist_ _infinitive_ συνᾰρέσθαι probably conjecture in [{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Bion Bucolicus “fragment” 8.8 (συνερᾶσθαι codices): see at {αἴρω},{ἄρνυμαι}:}}]_perfect_ _infinitive_ written συνῆρσθαι, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“BGU” 975.15 (1st c.AD), “PLips.” 27.15 (2nd c.AD)}}:—**take up together**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 945a39, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.659a}}; σ. λόγον μετά τινος **cast up** accounts.., {{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.Matt.18.23, “PLond.” 1.131r. 194 (1st c.AD),compare “PSI” 7.801.3 (2nd c.AD, _passive_), “Ostr.Bodl.” iii 336 (2nd-3rd c.AD, _passive_): _absolutely_, συναίρειν NT.Matt.18.24.}}
**2** ={συνάγω}, **gather in** a harvest, τὰ γενήματα {{Refs|“TAM” 2.245.9 (from Lycia):}} — _passive_, ὁ ἐπὶ τοῖς βαλανείοις συναιρόμενος ῥύπος **collected**, [{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.30 (variant{-αγόμενος}).}}]
**3** ὅπως συνάρωμεν διπλῆν εἰλαπίνην that we **may celebrate together** a double festival, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“BGU” 1080.9 (3rd c.AD).}}
**4** τῷ Καίσαρι συναίρει **espouses** Caesar's **cause**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 46.3 codices (perhaps -εται).}}
**II** _middle_, **take part in** a thing, with _genitive_ _of things_, συνάρασθαι τοῦ πολέμου, τοῦ κινδύνου, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.28, 4.10; σ. τισὶ τοῦ πολέμου 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 6.3}}: with _accusative_ _of things_, **help in bearing** or **undertaking**, ξυναίρεσθαι κίνδυνον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.71; τὰ πράγματα 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.24}}; also σ. Κύπριν **engage in** love **with** another, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 650; φόνον τινί 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 767 (troch.)}}; σ. τὴν Χάριν τινός **espoused his cause**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 45.15}}; συνάρασθαι εἰς τὸ αὐτό **co-operate**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Atheniensium” 2.2; μηδενὸς ὑμῶν μηδὲν συναραμένου 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “Prooemia” 41, compare 33}}; σ. τινί **with** one, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Galba” 18, etc.; τινὶ ἐς ἀποικίαν 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 3.1.7; πρὸς οὐδὲν αὐτῷ συνήρατο 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 37.49}}; ἐπί τινα in attacking him, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Comp.Dion. Brut.” 3}} **help, assist**, ταῖς ἀναγωγαῖς τοῦ πύου {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 11.683, compare 6.265; εἰς εὐτροφίαν τοῖς νεύροις [prev. work]209.}}
**II.2** **raise** or **use in helping**, οὐ συναίρεται δόρυ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 495}}; πᾶν ὅ τι ἔχομεν σ. τῷ κάλλει **enlist** all we have **in the service of** beauty, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Charid.” 12.}}
**II.3** τῶν σκελῶν σ. **catch by both** legs, trip up, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lysander” 15.}}
**III** _middle_ {{Refs 1st c.AD+|in signification 1.1, σ. λογάριον “PFay.” 109.6 (1st c.AD),compare “POxy.” 113.27 (2nd c.AD).}}
**IV** _middle_, **annul jointly with** another, ἐφ᾽ ᾧ συναρεῖταί μοι ἣν ἔχει ἡμῶν συνοικεσίου συγγραφήν {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 809.4 (2nd c.BC),compare “PRein.” 31.8 (2nd c.BC), “BGU” [prev. cited] (1st c.AD), “PLips.” [prev. cited] (2nd c.AD), “CPR” 23.17 (2nd c.AD)}}:— _passive_, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PRein.” 8.7 (2nd c.BC).}}

4870:
** συνᾰκολουθ-έω**,
**follow along with** or **closely, accompany**, τῇ στρατιᾷ {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.44, compare 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “pro Lycophrone” 6, “BGU” 1755.3 (1st c.BC), NT.Mark.14.51, etc.; σ. τινὶ οἴκαδε 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 43; πρὸς τὴν θεόν [prev. author] “Ra.” 400; μετὰ τοῦ στρατηγοῦ 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.146.}}
**2** **follow** with the mind, **attend to**, σ. ταῖς τύχαις {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1100b4}}; **follow** an argument **completely**, λόγῳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 25c, “Lg.” 629a}}; σ. τινί τι **follow** him **in** a matter, {{Refs|[prev. work] 792c}}; τισι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 188b26, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Sensu” 72.}}
**3** **to be correlated**, ἀλλήλοις {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 764b24, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 7.2.9.}}
**4** **follow** with the sense of **obeying**, with _dative_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 711c, 716b.}}
**5** **accrue, enure**, τινι {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“BGU” 906.22 (1st c.AD).}}
**II** of effects, **follow closely upon** the cause, πάντα σ. τῷ τοῦ παντὸς παθήματι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 274a; μετὰ τοῦ ῥήματος.. σ. τὰς ἡδονάς [prev. author] “R.” 464a; σ. τοῖς πλούτοις ἄνοια καὶ μετὰ ταύτης ἀκολασία 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 7.4, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 370b10, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 18(2).135.}}
**III** in the Logic of {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus}}, **follow necessarily with** a term, **be involved in** it, {{Refs|“APr.” 52b11}}; **to be mutually implied**, σ. αἱ ἀρχαί {{Refs|“Metaphorical” 1085a16.}}

4871:
** συνᾱλίζω**,
_aorist_
**A** συνήλισα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.176, -άλισα [prev. work] 125}}:—**bring together, collect, assemble**, τινας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.125; σ. ἐς τὴν ἀκρόπολιν τὰς γυναῖκας [prev. work] 176, compare 2.111; τοὺς ἐπιεικεστάτους.. πρὸς τὴν σκηνήν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.1.30}}:—_passive_, **come together, assemble**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.62, 5.15, 102, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.3.48, etc.; σ. εἰς τοὺς τελείους ἄνδρας [prev. author] “Cyr.” 1.2.15}}; of things, τὸ πλεῖστον ἐκ τοῦ μυελοῦ σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ὀστέων φύσιος” 15; βορβόρου περὶ αὐτὰ συναλισθέντος 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 763a33.}}

4872:
** συνανα-βαίνω**,
**go up with** or **together**, frequently of going into central Asia, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.6, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.4.16, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.146}}; τινι **with** one, {{NT+5th c.BC+|[prev. work]145, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.3.18; τινὶ εἰς Ἱεροσόλυμα NT.Mark.15.41; μετά τινος “OGI” 632.2 (Palmyra, 2nd c.AD)}}; σ. μέχρι Συήνης {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 2.5.12, compare 11.5.2}}; **pass upwards also**, διὰ τῶν ὀστῶν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 2.711}}; **ascend the sky with**, τῷ πόλῳ {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 8.14.}}
**2** σ. ἅρμα **mount** it **together**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Charid.” 19.}}

4876:
** συναντ-άω**
(compare συνάντομαι): _Epic dialect_ _imperfect_ **-ήντεον**{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 4.1486}}, _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _dual_ συναντήτην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.333}}: _future_ **-ήσω**{{LXX+NT|LXX.Isa.34.14, NT.Act.20.22}}: _aorist_ **-ήντησα**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.8.15}}: _perfect_ **-ήντηκα**{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.52.6, Ps.- 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Philopatr.” 1}}:—_middle_, once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad”, elsewhere only in late Prose (see. below)}}: _future_, {{LXX|LXX.Eccl.2.15, etc.}}:—**meet face to face**, of two persons, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey”}} [prev. cited]; of many persons, **meet together, assemble**, εἰς Φωκίδα Philipp. cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.157, compare “OGI” 56.5 (Canopus, 3rd c.BC)}}; **meet in battle**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.93.9: with _dative_, 3rd c.BC: Isyllus Lyricus 68.}}
**II** **meet with, encounter**, with _dative_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 787, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1187, “Pl.” 41, 44, etc.: _absolutely_, τὰ συνηντηκότα τῶν πλοίων 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.52.6; σ. συνάντησιν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 534 (troch.)}}; of heavenly bodies, {{Refs|“Catalogus Codicum Astrologorum” 7.204}}; **present oneself,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Zenon Papyri du Caire 300.6 (3rd c.BC)}}; φυγοδικοῦντας μὴ συναντῆσαί μοι {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PEnteux.” 65.4 (3rd c.BC),compare “PCair.Zen.” 179.9 (3rd c.BC)}}; εἰς.. **go to meet at** a place, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. work] 56.3, 247.2 (3rd c.BC)}}:—_middle_, ᾧ.. -αντήσωνται ἐν ὕῃ ἄνδρες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.134; πρός τινα “PHamb.” 25.11 (3rd c.BC).}}
**II.b** **fall in with, meet** a person's needs or wishes, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“SIG” 528.14 (Cos, 3rd c.BC), 590.43 (Milet., found at Cos, 2nd c.BC).}}
**II.2** with _dative_ _of things_, **come in contact with**, φόνῳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1210 (troch.).}}
**II.3** with _accusative_, **meet**, only among Asiatic Greeks, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Iamblichus Scriptor Eroticus 12, 2nd c.AD: Lesbonax Grammaticus 10.}}
**III** **befall, happen to** a person, τινι {{NT+1st c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Sulla” 2, NT.Act.20.22, 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 6.38: _absolutely_, μετρίως τὸ-τῆσαν οἴσει 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ παρρησίας” p.34 O., compare “Mort.” 37}}; of conception, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.44}}; εἰ ἔρρωσαι καὶ τἆλλά σοι κατὰ τρόπον σ. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PSI” 4.392.1 (3rd c.BC)}}:—_middle_, σ. τι παρά τινος {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 21.24.14, “SIG” 601.14 (Teos, 2nd c.BC).}}

4878:
** συναντι-λαμβάνομαι**,
_middle_, **help in gaining** a thing, τῆς ἐλευθερίας {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 14.8; τῶν τῇ πόλει συμφερόντων “SIG” 412.7 (Delph., 3rd c.BC)}}; τῆς θεραπείας {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ παρρησίας” p.19 O.}}; **assist in supporting**, τὴν ὁρμὴν τοῦ λαοῦ {{LXX|LXX.Num.11.17}}: _absolutely_, **render assistance**, περί τινων {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PHib.” 1.82.18 (3rd c.BC)}}; εἴς τι {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“OGI” 267.26 (Pergam., 3rd c.BC).}}
**II** with _dative_, **take part with, help**, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Exo.18.22, LXX.Psa.88(89).22, NT.Luke.10.40.}}

4879:
** συναπάγω** [ᾰγ],
**lead away with** or **together**, τινι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.3.23: _absolutely_, [prev. author] “HG” 5.1.23.}}
**2** **carry off with**, οἱ γλυκεῖς οἶνοι.. οὐ συναπάγουσιν ἑαυτοῖς τοὺς χολώδεις χυμούς {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.638.}}
**II** _passive_, τοὺς συναπαχθέντας ἡμῖν γεωργούς **arrested with** us, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 640.14 (3rd c.BC).}}
**II.2** _metaphorically_, **to be led away likewise,** {{NT|NT.Gal.2.13, NT.2Pet.3.17.}}
**II.3** ={συμπεριφέρομαι} (συμπεριφέρω {{NT|11.3), NT.Rom.12.16.}}

4884:
** συναρπάζω**,
_future_ **-άσω**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 535, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DDeor.” 8.1}}, **-άσομαι** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 437, 4th c.BC: Xenarchus Comicus 8}}:—**snatch and carry away with** one, **carry clean away**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 819, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1493 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.4.8, “PSI” 4.353.12 (3rd c.BC), 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.301, etc.; ξ. [τινὰ] βίᾳ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 195; βίᾳ ἐκ τῆς ὁδοῦ σ. τινάς 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 3.46, compare 12.96; πάντα σ. ὥσπερ θύελλα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1150}}; ὁ κρατῶν ἅμα πάντα σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.2.26; ἀετὸς τὸν λαγὼ σ. [prev. work] 2.4.19}}; **seize and retain**, οὐ δύνανται συναρπάζειν αἱ μῆτραι τὸν γόνον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus}}“Aër.” 21: _metaphorically_, **carry away with** one (by persuasive arguments), ξυναρπάσας στρατόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 531, compare 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Epigrammata” 32.5, 3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor 16.2, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “de Usu Partium” 3.10}}; οὐδένα ὑμῶν συναρπάζω I am not '**rushing**' you, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Diogenes Oenoandensis Epicureus 24}}; σ. ἑαυτὸν εἰς τὸ ἄνω, of mystical union with the One, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 5.3.4}}:—_passive_, **to be seized and carried off**, βία ξυναρπασθεῖσαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 498; σ. βουκόλων ὕπο [prev. author] “fragment” 659; by death, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “de Morte - Papiri Ercolanesi” 37.}}
**2** ξ. Χεῖρας **seize and pin** them **together**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1163, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator “Fragmenta” 75.4:}} —_middle_, ξ. τινὰ μέσον, of a wrestler, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 437.}}
**3** _metaphorically_, ξ. φρενί **seize** with the mind, **grasp**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 16, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 775; τὸ ῥηθέν Simylus Comicus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 4.18.4}}; σ. τὸ ζητούμενον, in arguing, **to be guilty of a petitio principii**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “JTr.” 38, 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 2.35, etc.}}; so συνήρπασται εἰς κτητικὴν σύνταξιν **is hastily concluded** to have a possessive force, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 165.9.}}
**4** **carry away, destroy all traces of**, τι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Dom.” 16.}}
**5** _passive_, of persons, συνηρπασμένοι **having been robbed,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|PRyl. 119.28 (1st c.AD).}}

4885:
** συναυξ-άνω**,
_imperfect_ συνηύξανε {{Refs|10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus}} see at {συνήκμαζε} (also _passive_ **-αυξάνομαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.7.6, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.72)}}, but usually **συναυξ-αύξω**, _aorist_ **-ηύξησα**{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.15.7, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Sertorius” 9, also -ηῦξα, _Doric dialect_ -αῦξα, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 32.1.7 (corr. Reiske), “Supp.epigram” (see. below)}}:—**increase** or **enlarge along with** or **together**, συναύξειν τῇ γῇ τὰ Χρήσιμα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.3.6 (in “hymnus ad Cererem” 267, Ignarra restored συνάξουσι)}}:—_passive_, **increase with** or **together, wax larger together with**, αὐξομένῳ τῷ σώματι συναύξονται καὶ αἱ φρένες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.134, compare 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 12, 53; εἰ μὴ ξυναύξοινθ᾽ οἱ πέπλοι τῷ σώματι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 544; ἀνδρὶ γενομένῳ ταῦτα πάντα συνηυξήθη 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.23, compare 1.7}}; πρός τι συμμέτρως συναυξάνεσθαι in proportion to, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 1.16; σπουδὴν.. προσφερόμενος εἰς τὸ συναύξεσθαι τὸν δῆμον “BCH” 48.3 (Prusa, 2nd c.BC(?)).}}
**2** **join** or **assist in increasing**, ἕξιν κακίης συναύξει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 184; συναύξειν οἴκους 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 3.10; συναύξει τὴν ἐνέργειαν ἡ ἡδονή 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1175a30, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Sensu” 18, “Ign.” 27, 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.29; τὰν ὑπάρχουσαν ὁμόνοιαν.. ἐπὶ πλεῖον συναύξησε “Klio” 15.41 (Delph., 3rd c.BC)}}; τὰν φιλίαν συναῦξε {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Supp.epigram” 2.270.6 ([prev. passage], 2nd c.BC)}}; συναύξοντες τὴν ἀρχὴν τῷ Κύρῳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.3.21; τὰς τῶν θεῶν τιμάς “Supp.epigram” 4.720.15 (Chalcedon, 3rd c.BC)}}; **join in exaggerating**, τι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.15.7, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 9.19.3.}}

4886:
** σύνδεσμ-ος**, ὁ,
irregular _plural_ σύνδεσμα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus (see. below)}}:—**that which binds together, bond of union, fastening**, ξ. ἦν.. τὰ ξύλα, τοῦ μὴ ἀσθενὲς εἶναι τὸ οἰκοδόμημα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.75}}; ἀραρότως σύνδεσμα χρυσὸς εἶχε the golden headband kept its **bonds** firmly fixed, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1193}}; ἁμμάτων σύνδεσμα **fastenings** of garments, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ba.” 697; οἱ περὶ [τὸ θύρωμα] σ. Aristeas 85}}; μελέων σύνδεσμα, of **sinews** or **ligaments**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 199 (_anapaest meter_), compare 5th-4th c.BC: Timaeus Locrus Philosophus 100b, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 652a16, “IA” 712a2, NT.Col.2.19, 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.57, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 18(1).734.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, **bond of union**; ὁ σ. τῆς πόλεως **the bond that keeps** the state **together**, of good citizens, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 520a, compare “Plt.” 310a; νόμος ὁ βοηθῶν.. τῷ τῆς πόλεως συνδέσμῳ [prev. author] “Lg.” 921c; σ. τοῦ οὐρανοῦ [prev. author] “R.” 616c; σ. τὰ τέκνα δοκεῖ εἶναι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1162a27; τὴν ἀγάπην, ὅ ἐστι σ. τῆς τελειότητος NT.Col.3.14.}}
**3** Grammars, **conjunction**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1407a20}}; σ. ἓν ποιεῖ τὰ πολλά{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. work] 1413b32, compare “Rh.Al.” 1434b13, “Po.” 1456b38, 3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 2.45, 2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus 634.6, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “Conj.” passim, 2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor “περὶ ἰδεῶν” 2.7.}}
**II** ={σύνδεσις}{{Refs 5th c.BC+|1, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epinomis” 984c; συνδέσμῳ ἕν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Posteriora” 93b36, “Metaphorical” 1045a13, compare “Int.” 17a9.}}
**III** **that which is bound together, bundle**, ἐπιστολῶν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 4.12.6.}}
**IV** **conspiracy**, {{LXX|LXX.2Ki.11.14, +others}}
**V** **sodomy**,{{LXX|LXX.1Ki.14.24.}}
**VI** Astron., **node**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Eudoxus Astronomus “Ars astronomica” 18.13, 2nd c.AD: Theon Smyrnaeus Philosophus p.194 H., 2nd c.AD: Cleomedes Astronomus 2.5, 1st c.BC: Ptolemaeus Epigrammaticus “Alm.” 6.5.}}
**VI.2** Σ. **the Knot**, i.e. the star **a** Piscium, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Hipparchus Astronomicus 1.11.20, 1st c.BC: Geminus Astronomicus 3.7, [prev. author] “Calend.” 10, 1st c.BC: Ptolemaeus Epigrammaticus “Alm.” 8.1.}}
**VI.3** Astrology texts, **connexion** of heavenly bodies, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 163.13, +others}}; of the moon, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Paulus Alexandrinus Astrologus “S.” 3.}}

4887:
** συνδέω**,
**bind** or **tie together**, of two or more things, συνέδησα πόδας δεινοῖο πελώρου {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.168; σὺν δὲ πόδας χεῖράς τε δέον 22.189; οἶνος σ. πόδας χεῖράς τε γλῶσσάν τε νόον τε 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Fragmenta” 121; τοὺς πόδας καὶ τὰς χεῖρας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 4c}}; σ. γαύλους **bind** them **together, side by side**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.97, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.22.9}}; δέλτον λύειν καὶ σ. **fasten** it **up**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 110}}; **act as binding material**, ὁ συνδέων πηλός {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“CPR” 232.17 (2nd-3rd c.AD):}} — _passive_, τὰς χεῖρας συνεδέθησαν **had** their hands **tied together**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demades Orator 13}}; ἰσχία μὴ συνδεδεμένα flanks not **well-knit**, of dogs, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 4.1, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 873a33.}}
**2** of persons, **bind hand and foot**, ὁππότε μιν ξυνδῆσαι Ὀλύμπιοι ἤθελον ἄλλοι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.399, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.119, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 62, “Ph.” 1016, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 238, etc.}}; λαγὼς αὐτὸς σ. ἑαυτόν **entangles** itself, {{Refs|X “Cyr.” 1.6.40:}} —_passive_, συνδεδεμένος **constrained, cramped**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Philostrati majoris imagines” 2.21.}}
**2.b** **bind up** a wound, σφενδόνῃ **with.. **, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.599.}}
**3** **bind up with, combine closely**, σάρκας ὀστοῖς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 84a, compare 73b, “Smp.” 202e, “Tht.” 160b; also τι ἀπό τινος 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Syr.D.” 29}}; of parts growing together, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 1.40.}}
**4** generally, **bind together, unite**, [ἰσότης] φίλους φίλοις πόλεις τε πόλεσι συμμάχους τε συμμάχοις σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 538; τὸ κοινὸν συνδεῖ τὰς πόλεις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 875a; ἡδονῆς καὶ λύπης κοινωνία συνδεῖ [prev. author] “R.” 462b; σ. καὶ συνέχειν [prev. author] “Phd.” 99c}}; σ. τινὰ πενίᾳ **bind** him to.., {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 3.49.}}
**5** **connect**, opposed to διαζευγνύω, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Conjunctionibus;” 214.6, +others}}
**II** _middle_, σύνδησαι πέπλους **gird up thy** robes, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 832 (Lyric poetry, Reiske for πέπλοις).}}
**II.2** **have** things **bound together**, Ti. _Locrian dialect_{{Refs 4th c.AD+|99a, 4th c.AD: Themistius Sophista “Orationes” 4.59a.}}
**II.3** **unite themselves, form a union**, πρὸς παίδων γέννησιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 310b, cf. Betion cited in 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 4.54.}}

4888:
** συνδοξάζω**,
**join in approving**, νόμοι συνδεδοξασμένοι ὑπὸ πάντων {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1310a15.}}
**2** **agree with**, τῷ σώματι {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 1.2.3, 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 3.1.123, 5th-6th c.AD: Marinus Biographus “Procl.” 21.}}
**II** **glorify** or **extol jointly**, in _passive_, {{NT|NT.Rom.8.17.}}

4889:
** σύνδουλ-ος**, ὁ, ἡ,
**fellow-slave**, _masculine_, {{NT+5th c.BC+|[prev. author = 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus] “Ion” 1109, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 745 (_anapaest meter_), 5th c.BC: Theopompus Comicus 32.8, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator “Fragmenta” 331 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 5.56, NT.Matt.18.29, etc.; _feminine_, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.134, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 65, etc.}}; also _feminine_ συνδούλη, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.110, 2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 3.6.}}
**2** _metaphorically_ in **NT,** {{NT|NT.Col.1.7, NT.Rev.6.11, +others}} (The statement of Moeris {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|p.273 P., ὁμόδουλος Ἀττικῶς, σύνδουλος Ἑλληνικῶς, is incorrect: 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 3.82}} distinguishes ς. 'slave of the same master' from ὁμόδουλος 'companion in slavery'.)

4890:
** συνδρομ-ή**, ἡ,
**tumultuous concourse** of people, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Cephisodorus Comicus cited in 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1411a29, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.67.2 (_plural_), LXX.Jdt.10.18, NT.Act.21.30}}; ἐπί τινα, κατά τινων, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 3.71, 15.90; σ. τῶν ὄχλων εἰς τὴν ἐκκλησίαν 2nd-1st c.BC: Posidonius Historicus 36 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus}}; ἀπὸ συνδρομῆς **tumultuously**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 13.87.}}
**2** of things, στενὴ πορθμοῦ σ. (compare {συνδρομάς}) {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Lycophron Tragicus 649}}; σ. αἵματος εἰς τὸν πληγέντα τόπον a **determination** of blood, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 889b30; σ. θερμοῦ 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.695a}}; **combination**, κέκληται ἡ σ. τούτων καυλός {{Refs 1st c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.9; σ. ἀγαθῶν 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 5.3.7}}; ἡς. τοῦ λόγου its **conclusion, moral,** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|Anthologia Graeca 9.203 (9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc. or 5th c.AD: Leo Philosophus Epigrammaticus)}}; especially in Medicine texts, **concurrence** of symptoms, 'clinical picture', {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 11.59, 2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “ὀξέων νούσων θεραπευτικόν” 1.10.}}
**2.b** **contraction** of a muscle, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Antyllus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 45.15.5, “Catalogus Codicum Astrologorum” 8(3).147 (_plural_)}}; of the prepuce, {{Refs 7th c.AD+|7th c.AD: Paulus Aegineta Medicus 6.55.}}
**3** in Rhet., **provisional concession** of an adversary's standpoint, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor “περὶ ἰδεῶν” 2.1, 7, 2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor “τέχναι ῥητορικαί” 1p.491S.}}

4892:
** συνέδρ-ιον**, τό,
**council**, σ. κατασκευάζειν, συνάγειν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 317d, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.89; ὁμογνώμονες τοῦ σ. πάντες “IG” 14.952.28 (Acragas, 3rd c.BC)}}; **meeting**, μεταπεμφθέντων εἰς κοινὸν σ. τῶν.. δεκανῶν {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 27.31 (2nd c.BC)}}; of a **council of war**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.1.31, etc.}}; of the Areopagus, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.91, 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.54; τὰ σ., of the 600}} together with the Areopagus, {{Refs|“IG” 22.3640}}; of a **board of trade**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 58.8}}; of the Roman {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Senate”, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.11.1, etc.; the Carthaginian “Senate”, [prev. author] 1.31.8}}; the Jewish **Sanhedrin,** {{NT+4th c.AD+|NT.Matt.5.22, etc. (also of local “Councils” attached to synagogues, [prev. work]10.17, NT.Mark.13.9, “Codex Justinianus [4th-6th c.AD]” 1.9.17); the “Senate” at Constantinople, 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 20.37}}; τὸ σεμνότατον σ. τῆς γερουσίας, at Thasos, {{Refs|“IG” 12(8).388; τὸ σ. σ. τῶν γερόντων “IGRom.” 4.782 (from Apamea)}}; τὸ χωρίον ἐν ᾧ σ. ἦν αὐτῷ τῶν πολιτῶν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.332}}; frequently of a **congress** of Allies or Confederates, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.56, 75, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.1.39, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.22, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.70, 3.58, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 16.89, etc.; τὸ σ. τῶν Ἑλλάνων “IG” 12(3).1259.4 (Smyrna, 4th c.BC)}}; ἁμῶν (i.e. τῶν Ἀμφικτιόνων) {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“OGI” 234.17 (Delph., 3rd c.BC),compare “SIG” 613.10 ([prev. passage], 2nd c.BC)}}; τὸ Ἑλληνικὸν σ. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Aristides” 19.}}
**2** **place of meeting, council-chamber**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.79, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.4.23, “POxy.” 717.8 (1st c.BC)}}; ἐν σ. in **court**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 9.6; ἐνεπρήσθη τὰ σ. τῶν Πυθαγορείων 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.39.1.}}

4893:
** συνείδησις**, εως, ἡ,
**knowledge shared with another**, τῶν ἀλγημάτων (in a midwife) {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.4.}}
**2** **communication, information**, εὑρήσεις σ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PPar.” p.422 (2nd c.AD)}}; σ. εἰσήνεγκαν τοῖς κολλήγαις αὐτῶν {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“POxy.” 123.13 (3rd-4th c.AD).}}
**3** **knowledge**, λῦε ταῦτα πάντα μὴ διαλείψας ἀγαθῇ σ. (variant{ἀγαθῇ τύχῃ}) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιστολαί” 1.}}
**4** **consciousness, awareness**, [τῆς αὑτοῦ συστάσεως] {{NT+5th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 3.43, compare 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.140 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, NT.2Cor.4.2, 5.11, NT.1Pet.2.19; τῆς κακοπραγμοσύνης 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 297, compare 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 4.65, NT.Heb.10.2; κατὰ συνείδησιν ἀτάραχοι διαμενοῦσι 2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “Belopoeica” 73}}; **inner consciousness**, ἐν σ. σου βασιλέα μὴ καταράσῃ [{{LXX+NT|LXX.Eccl.10.20; in NT.1Cor.8.7 συνειδήσει is _falsa lectio_ for{συνηθείᾳ}.}}]
**5** **consciousness of right** or **wrong doing, conscience**, Periander and {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Bias Lyricus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 3.24.11,12, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Am.” 49}}; ἐὰν ἐγκλήματός τινος ἔχῃ σ. {{Refs|Anonymus “Oxy.” 218}} (**a**) ii 19; βροτοῖς ἅπασιν ἡ σ. θεός {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Μονόστιχοι - Fragmenta Comicorum Graecorum” 654, cf. LXX.Wis.17.11, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Thucydide” 8 (but perhaps interpolated); σ. ἀγαθή NT.Act.23.1}}; ἀπρόσκοπος πρὸς τὸν θεόν{{NT+2nd c.AD+|[prev. work] 24.16; καθαρά NT.1Tim.3.9, “POsl.” 17.10 (2nd c.AD)}}; κολαζομένους κατὰ συνείδησιν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 210.1; θλειβομένη τῇ σ. περὶ ὧν ἐνοσφίσατο “PRyl.” 116.9 (2nd c.AD)}}; τὸν.. θεὸν κεχολωμένον ἔχοιτο καὶ τὴν ἰδίαν σ. {{Refs|“Ath.Mitt.” 24.237 (from Thyatira)}}; **conscientiousness,** {{Refs 6th c.AD+|Arch.Papyrus 3.418.13 (6th c.AD)}}.--Senses 4 and 5 sometimes run one into the other, see {{NT|NT.1Cor.8.7, 10.27f}}
**6** **complicity, guilt, crime**, περὶ τοῦ πεφημίσθαι αὐτὴν ἐν σ. τοιαύτῃ {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Supp.epigram” 4.648.13 (Lydia, 2nd c.AD).}}

4894:
** σύνοιδα**,
_perfect_ with _present_ sense, _1st pers._ _plural_ σύνισμεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 232c}}, _Ionic dialect_ συνοίδαμεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.60}}, _3rd.pers._ _plural_ συνίσᾱσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 93 (_anapaest meter_), 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.113, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.1.9, etc. (rarely συνοίδασι 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 11.1, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 27.9.11); _imperative_ σύνισθι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 870}}; _infinitive_ ξυνειδέναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 266:}}_pluperfect_ with _imperfect_ sense, συνῄδειν, _Attic dialect_ συνῄδη, dual συνῄστην, _plural_ -ῇσμεν, -ῇστε, -ῇσαν, _Ionic dialect2nd pers._ _plural_ συνῃδέᾰτε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.58}}: _future_ συν (ξυν-) είσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 17, “V.” 999, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.4.17 (rarely συνειδήσω 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.16}}, and _aorist_ _participle_ -ειδήσας{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ παρρησίας” p.32 O.)}}:—**know** something **about** a person, especially **as a potential witness for** or **against** him, ἢ (i.e. Δίκη) σιγῶσα σύνοιδε τὰ γιγνόμενα πρό τ᾽ ἐόντα {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 4.15}}; τά τοι ἐγὼ καὶ ἀμφότερα συνειδὼς ἔχω μαρτυρέειν to both of which I, **knowing them true of** you, can testify, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.24; εἰ τέοισί τι χρηστὸν συνῄδεε πεποιημένον [prev. author] 8.113; ἐπαινεόντων τούτους τοῖσί τι καὶ συνῃδέατε [prev. author] 9.58}}; τὰ Μηδικὰ καὶ ὅσα αὐτοὶ σύνιστε our other services **to** which **you can testify**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.73; ἂ ξύνισμεν τοῖσιν ἵπποις βουλόμεσθ᾽ ἐπαινέσαι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 595 (troch.); συνοίδαμεν ὑμῖν ὑπὸ τὸν παρεόντα τόνδε πόλεμον ἐοῦσι πολλὸν προθυμοτάτοισι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.60}}; ὑπολαμβάνων παρ᾽ ὑμῶν ἑκάστῳ τὸ συνειδὸς ὑπάρχειν μοι believing that I can rely on your **acknowledgement** of my services, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.110}}; τί μοι σύνοισθα τοιοῦτον εἰργασμέν; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 4.62; σύνοιδα τῷ μειρακίῳ κοσμίῳ τὸν πρότερον ὄντι χρόνον ἀεί 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Σαμία” 57; πότερον οὐ συνοίδασιν αὐτῷ ποιοῦντι τὰ δίκαια 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 27.9.11}}; ξυνειδὼς οὐ φράσει; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 330; ἵνα τούτῳ ταῦτα συνειδῶμεν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 348b; ἐρῶ.. ἂ σύνοιδα αὐτῷ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.7.1}}; οὐκ αἰσχυνοῦμαι.. εἰπεῖν (variant) ἅπασιν ὅσα σύνοιδ᾽ αὐτῷ κακά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 200; ξύνοιδ᾽ ἄντροισιν αἰσχύνην τινά 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 288}}; θνῄσκοντι συνείσῃ (conjecture Reiske for συνοίσῃ) **thou wilt witness** my death, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1085 (Lyric poetry); διὰ δικαιοσύνην, τήν οἱ ἄλλην συνῄδεε ἐοῦσαν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.164; τοιοῦτον αὐτοῖς Ἄρεος εὔβουλον πάγον ἐγὼ ξυνῄδη χθόνιον ὄνθ᾽ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 948}}; δύ᾽ ἡμῶν ἢ τρία κακὰ ξυνειδὼς εἶπε δρώσας μυρία (i.e. Εὐριπίδης) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 475, compare 553; ἀφανίζει τὸν παῖδα, ὃς συνῄδει περὶ τῶν χρημάτων 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 17.11, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 210; τῆς ἁρπαγῆς τοῦ παιδὸς εἰ ξύνοισθά τι, ταχέως λέγειν χρή 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 74.3}}; καὶ τίνα σύνοισθά μοι καλουμένῃ βροτῶ; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 216; σύνοιδ᾽ Ὀρέστην πολλά σ᾽ ἐκπαγλουμένην [prev. work] 217}}; σύνοιδα τοῖς πλείστοις αὐτῶν ἥκιστα χαίρουσι I **can bear witness** that most of them are far from pleased.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 7.50}}; with a mixture of _dative_ and _accusative_ _construction_, συνίσασι γὰρ αὐτῷ.. καθιστάμενον, ἐκ δὲ τούτων.. δυνάμενον {{Refs 7th c.BC+|[prev. author] 15.120; ἐγώ σοι σ... ἀνιστάμενον καὶ.. βαδίζοντα καὶ ἀναπείθοντα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 3.7; frequently with _reflexive_ _pronoun_ in _dative_, ἔμ᾽ αὔτᾳ τοῦτο σύνοιδα 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 15}}; ἐξ ὧν αὐτὸς σύνοισθα σαυτῷ ἐν τῇ τῶν γραμμάτων μαθήσει from your **experience of** yourself.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 206a}}; σύνοιδ᾽ ἐμαυτῇ πολλὰ (δείν᾽) {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 477, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.9.6, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 331a; ξυνειδέναι τί μοι δοκεῖς σαυτῷ καλόν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 184; πολλὴν ἑαυτοῖς συνειδέναι ἀσέβειαν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Apologia Socratis” 24; συνειδὼς ἐμαυτῷ ἀμαθίαν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 235c; τὴν πατρίδα, εἰς ἢν τοσαύτην εὔνοιαν ἐμαυτῷ σύνοιδα 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “Epistulae” 2.20; σ. ἑαυτοῖς ἄγνοιαν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1095a25; σ. αὑτῷ τὴν δειλίαν [prev. author] “HA” 618a26; μηδὲν ἑαυτοῖς ἄτοπον συνειδέναι 5th-4th c.BC: Socratis et Socraticorum Epistulae cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 3.24.13, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.59, LXX.Job.27.6, NT.1Cor.4.4}}; συνειδότες αὑτοῖς **with full consciousness**, {{Refs|Polystr. p.15}} W.:—with _participle_
**a** in _nominative_, πῶς οὖν ἐμαυτῷ τοῦτ᾽ ἐγὼ ξυνείσομαι, φεύγοντ᾽ ἀπολύσας ἄνδρ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 999; ἡνίκ᾽ ἄν τις ἐσθλὸς ὢν αὑτῷ συνειδῇ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 931; ὅστις τούτων σύνοιδεν αὑτῷ παρημεληκώς 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.5.7; σύνισμεν ἡμῖν αὐτοῖς ἀπὸ παίδων ἀρξάμενοι ἀσκηταὶ ὄντες τῶν καλῶν κἀγαθῶν ἔργων [prev. author] “Cyr.” 1.5.11; συνειδέναι σαυτῷ δοκεῖς οὐπώποτ᾽ ἀμελήσας αὐτῶν [prev. work] 1.6.4, compare 3.3.38, “HG” 2.3.12; οὔτε μέγα οὔτε σμικρὸν σύνοιδα ἐμαυτῷ σοφὸς ὤν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 21b; ὅταν καὶ μηδὲν σαυτῷ συνειδῇς ἐξαμαρτάνων 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.79.}}
**b** in _dative_, σύνοιδα ἐμαυτῷ ἀντιλέγειν οὐ δυναμένῳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 216b; σύνισμεν ἡμῖν αὐτοῖς κηλουμένοις ὑπ᾽ αὐτῆς [prev. author] “R.” 607c; τὸ συνειδέναι πεπρακόσιν αὑτοῖς τὰ πράγματα 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.208; συνειδόθ᾽ αὑτῷ φαῦλα διαπεπραγμένῳ 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 229.}}
**2** with _dative_ _of things_, **know** something **about** a thing, τοῖς διὰ τῶν εἰκότων τὰς ἀποδείξεις ποιουμένοις λόγοις σύνοιδα οὖσιν ἀλαζόσιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 92d; διὰ τὸ συνειδέναι τοῖς σφετέροις πράγμασι τετρυ[μ]μένοις καὶ κάμνουσιν ἤδη τῷ πολέμῳ 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.62.7; σε.. συνειδότα τοῖς πρὸς ἐμὲ σοὶ πεπολιτευμένοις 3rd-2nd c.BC: Phalaris Epistolographus “Epistulae - Epistolographi” 109}}; καιρὸς.. σὲ εἰπεῖν ἃ σύνοισθα τῷ βίῳ ἑκάστῳ what **you know about** each life, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Gall.” 15.}}
**II** **share the knowledge of** something **with** somebody, **to be implicated in** or **privy to** it, οὐδὲ ξυνῄδει σοί τις ἔκθεσιν τέκνο; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 956; δουλοῖ γὰρ ἄνδρα,.. ὅταν ξυνειδῇ μητρὸς ἢ πατρὸς κακά [prev. author] “Hipp.” 425}}; ξύνοιδε δ᾽ οὔτις οἰκετῶν νόσον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 40; συνίσασί σοι πάντα ὅσα ἔπραξας 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.1.9; ξυνίσασ᾽ εὐναὶ.. ὅσα θρηνῶ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 93 (_anapaest meter_); πλῆθος ὃ ξυνῄδει 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.68; ξυνειδώς τις [prev. passage]; ὁ συνειδὼς καὶ μὴ φράζων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 742b; οἱ ξυνειδότες σφίσι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.20; οἱ συνειδότες πεποιηκότι τι δεινόν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1382b6; σύνοισθά που καὶ αὐτὸς ὅτι.. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 257d; σύνισμεν ὡς.. [prev. author] “Sph.” 232c; σύνοιδέ μοι εἰ ἐπιορκῶ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.6.18}}; συνειδέναι δὲ (i.e. τὰς μαντηΐας) καὶ τοὺς Πυθίους {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.57; συνειδυίας καὶ τῆς γυναικός NT.Act.5.2:— with}}_participle_
**II.a** in _dative_, μήτε τῳ ξυνειδέναι τὸ πρᾶγμα βουλεύσαντι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 266; εἰ μὴ συνῄδη Σωκράτει τε καὶ Ἀγάθωνι δεινοῖς οὖσι περὶ τὰ ἐρωτικά 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 193e.}}
**II.b** in _accusative_, ἀνδράποδα, ἂ συνῄδει τὴν γυναῖκα.. θάνατον μηχανωμένην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.9, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 773b, 870d, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 49.58, 59.67, 61.23.}}
**III** **know well**, αὐτὸς ξυνειδώς, ἢ μαθὼν ἄλλου πάρ; as **from his** own **knowledge**, or.. ? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 704}}; so also (unless there is ellipse of _reflexive_ _pronoun_) σύνοιδα δείν᾽ εἰργασμένος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 396; σύνοισθά γ᾽ εἰς ἔμ᾽ οὐκ εὔορκος ὤν [prev. author] “Med.” 495.}}
**IV** with _dative_ _of things_. **to be privy to**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“BGU” 1141.50 (1st c.BC)}}; τῷ φόνῳ {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Scholia 2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor in Rhetores Graeci 4.355 W.}}
**V** τὸ συνειδός **complicity**, τὸ σ. τοῦ πράγματος {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Publicola” 4}}; **consciousness**, τοῦ ἐνδεοῦς {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.84d.}}
**V.2** **conscience**, ὑπὸ συνειδότος ἐπαρρησιάζετο ἀγαθοῦ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 7.10.10, compare 3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 6.7, 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 1.10.5, Chor. p.38 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus}}
**VI** ὡς ἂν συνειδῇς as you may **think proper**, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 13.2.}}

4895:
** σύνειμι**,
(εἶμι **ibo) go** or **come together, assemble**, ἐς χῶρον ἕνα ξυνιόντες ἵκοντο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.446; ἐς τὠυτό 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.62; ἐς τὸν Ἰσθμόν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.10, compare “SIG” 835 A 4 (Delph., 2nd c.AD)}}; συνιόντος ὄχλου πολλοῦ {{NT|NT.Luke.8.4.}}
**2** in hostile sense, **meet in battle**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.393, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 686; ἐς μέσον.. συνίτην μεμαῶτε μάχεσθαι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.120; ἔριδι ξυνιόντες 20.66, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 705; ἔριδος πέρι θυμοβόροιο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.476; σ. ἐς τὴν μάχην 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.80}}; of states, **engage in war**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.8.}}
**3** in peaceable sense, **come together, meet to consult** or **deliberate**,{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 15, 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 126, etc.; σ. περὶ νόμων θέσεως 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1298a17}}; of a conspirator, σ. τοῖς φυγάσιν ἐπὶ καταλύσει τοῦ δήμου {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.94, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.144}}; also of festive meetings, συνόδους συνιέναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 197d.}}
**3.b** of the council, σύλλογον ὃν εἶπες συνιέναι {{Refs|[prev. author] “Lg.” 962c.}}
**4** σ. εἰς τὴν κοινωνίαν, of marriage, {{Refs|[prev. work] 773a}}; of copulation of animals, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 540a10.}}
**II** of things, **gather**, σ. ἀήρ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 49c; τὸ ὑγρόν 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 2.19.3}}; of clouds, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 364b33}}; opposed to χωρίζεσθαι, {{Refs|[prev. author] “GC” 327b28}}; σ. πρὸς αὑτήν **recur**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 58a, compare 76a.}}
**II.2** of money, **come together, come in**, of revenue, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.64, 4.1.}}
**II.3** **to be contracted**, σ. καὶ ψύχεσθαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 342a19.}}
**II.4** of stars, **come into conjunction**, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Manetho Astrologus 2.423, +others}}; of the moon, συνιούσης, opposed to αὐξομένης, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Lydus, Joannes Laurentius Historicus “de Mensibus” 3.11.}}

4896:
** σύνειμι**
(εἰμί **sum**), _future_ -έσομαι, _Doric dialectfuture_ **-εσσέομαι**{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“SIG” 526.19 (Itanos, 3rd c.BC): Elean _3rd.pers._ _plural_}}_present_ _optative_ συνέαν{{Refs 6th c.BC+|[prev. work]9 (Olympia, 6th c.BC)}}:— **to be with, be joined with**, ἔμελλον ἔτι ξυνέσεσθαι ὀϊζυῖ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.270}}; ξ. ὀνείρασιν **to be haunted by** dreams, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 177}}; σ. νόσῳ, ={νοσεῖν}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 303}}; κακοῖς πολλοῖς ξυνοῦσα **acquainted with.. **, {{Refs|[prev. author] “El.” 600}}; τῷ κόπῳ ξ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 321 (Lyric poetry); γνώμαις καὶ μερίμναις [prev. author] “Nu.” 1404}}; [πράγμασι] **to be engaged in** business, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ra.” 959; ξ. ᾧπερ ἥδεσθον βίῳ [prev. author] “fragment” 583}}; [μέρει πολέμου] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.18}}; τρυφερῷ βίῳ σ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Kith.fragment” 1.9}}; γεωργίᾳ σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 15.12}}; εὐωχίαις, ἡδοναῖς, δείμασι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 586a, 586b, “Lg.” 791b}}; ἀπορίᾳ, εὐδαιμονία, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Sat.” 11, “Bis Acc.” 3: reversely, ὅτῳ τὸ μὴ καλὸν ξύνεστι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 372 (Lyric poetry); ὅτῳ γάμοι ξυνόντες ηὑρέθησαν ἀνόσιοι [prev. author] “OC” 946; ἐμοὶ ξύνεστιν ἐλπίς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 682; εἴ μοι ξυνείη.. μοῖρα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 863 (Lyric poetry): _absolutely_, ἆται ἀεὶ ξυνοῦσαι [prev. author] “OC” 1244 (Lyric poetry); τὰ πάλαι νοσήματα ξυνόντα [prev. author] “Aj.” 338; ὁ χρόνος ξυνὼν μακρός [prev. author] “OC” 7.}}
**II** **have intercourse with, live with**, τοῖς φονεῦσι τοῦ πατρός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “El.” 264, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 897.7 (_anapaest meter_), etc.; μετά τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 504, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 195b, etc.}}; σ. ἐμαυτῷ **live alone**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 6.2}}; φιλικῶς, οἰκείως ξ. τινί, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “An.” 6.6.35, “HG” 7.3.5; σ. ἀλλήλοις ἐν τῷ πότῳ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 347c: also ξυνῆμεν.. ἐγώ τε καὶ σύ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 236}}; οἱ συνόντες τινί, of fellow-travellers, {{NT+5th c.BC+|NT.Act.22.11: _absolutely_, τὸν νεανίσκον συνὼν διέφθορεν 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 337.}}
**II.2** of a woman, **live with** a husband, ={συνοικέω}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.9, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 276, 611, etc.}}; and then, merely, **have sexual intercourse**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 619 (_anapaest meter_), 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1262a33, “PSI” 1.64.19 (1st c.BC)}}, etc.; of animals, **copulate**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 540a13.}}
**II.3** **attend, associate with**, a teacher, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.8, 24, etc.}}; also of the teacher, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 3.1.14, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 151a, etc.}}; of a fellowpupil, ἐμοὶ συνών ποτε περὶ μαθήματα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 16.684}}; also of a **follower** in war, ξ. Βρασίδᾳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 475 (Lyric poetry)}}; οἱ συνόντες **followers, partisans, associates, disciples**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.68, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 25e, “Tht.” 168a, +others}}; **guests**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1300, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 1.15, etc.}}; **comrades** in war, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 8.2.2}}; Δίκη ξυνοῦσα φωτί **attending on, favouring**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 671, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 275, etc.}}; **accompany**, οὐδὲ τῷ ὀρέγεσθαι τὸ εὔκαιρον σύνεστι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.38, compare 26.}}
**II.4** **have dealings with**, τινι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.83}}; σ. ἵπποις **have to do with** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 25b.}}
**II.5** **take part in, attend**, συνόδοις {{Refs|“Rev.Arch.” 22(1925).62 (from Callatis)}}; ὑπογραψάντων πάντων τῶν συνόντων all the **members** of the σύνοδος, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Sammelb.” 7457.48 (2nd c.BC).}}
**II.6** _absolutely_, αἴ κα.. μὴ συννῇ (συνῇ) γνήσια τέκνα if there **are** not **in addition** children of the blood, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Leg.Gort.” 10.41; ὅπου κεφαλαλγία σύνεστι 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 16.662.}}
**III** of heavenly bodies, **to be in conjunction**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Manetho Astrologus 1.78, +others, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 19.552.}}

4898:
** συνέκδημ-ος**, ὁ,
**fellow-traveller,** {{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.Acts.19.29, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Vita” 14, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Otho” 5, 4th c.AD: Palaephatus Paradoxographus 45}}; = Latin _comes_, συνέγδημος Μεσσάλλα τοῦ ἀνθυπάτου {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“OGI” 494.13 (Milet., 2nd c.AD)}}: _Doric dialect_ _nominative_ _plural_ **συνέγδᾱμοι**, of private persons **accompanying** a public mission, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“IG” 12(8).186.9 (Samothrace, 1st c.BC).}}
**2** σ. συντάγματα **portable** handbooks, **vade-mecums**, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|7th c.AD: Paulus Aegineta Medicus “Prooem.”; σ. κολλούριον 6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 7.103.}}
